Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First, two quick clarifying points so what I prepare is accurate:

- Your note lists cash = $1.2M, runway = 6 months, burn = $62K/month. Those three aren’t internally consistent (6 months at $62K/mo implies only ~$372K cash). Which of these is correct — the cash balance ($1.2M), the stated runway (6 months), or the $62K burn? Or is the $62K/month net burn after revenue?  
- Do you want: (A) a one‑page finance narrative for leadership, (B) an internal memo to the company, (C) slide bullets for an investor/board deck, or (D) a modeled 12‑month cash / P&L scenario (best/base/worst)? I can do any — tell me which to start with.

Below are actionable recommendations you can use immediately and language for the finance narrative. I’ll keep numbers high level until you confirm the cash/runway/burn.

Immediate (0–90 days) — protect runway and demonstrate discipline
- Reconcile runway math and make that the headline metric (cash / net burn). Present base and conservative scenarios.  
- Hiring & spend freeze: pause all non‑critical hires and discretionary spend. Target 10–20% near‑term savings on SG&A.  
- Cut low ROI marketing & projects: pause programs with high CAC or long payback. Reallocate to highest ROI channels.  
- Reduce cash outflows: push for longer vendor payment terms, renegotiate contracts, and consolidate subscriptions.  
- Accelerate cash inflows: require or incentivize advance payments, shorten invoicing cycles, and prioritize collection of receivables.  
- Temporary salary adjustments or reduced hours (with legal/HR review) where necessary — present as short term to preserve jobs.  
- Freeze capital expenditures and new product launches that need cash.

Margin improvement levers (medium term, 3–12 months)
- Pricing optimization: test modest price increases (2–7%) or introduce tiered pricing with higher‑margin tiers. Run A/B tests on pricing for segments.  
- Product mix: promote higher margin products/services; bundle low‑margin services into recurring packages to increase gross margin.  
- Cost of goods sold (COGS): renegotiate supplier pricing, consolidate vendors, source lower cost inputs, and reduce returns/waste.  
- Move fixed to variable cost: outsource non‑core functions or convert headcount to contractors where feasible to improve flexibility.  
- Automation & tooling: invest selectively in automation (billing, onboarding, ad ops) to reduce variable labor per unit of revenue.  
- Delivery efficiency: standardize processes, create playbooks, and reduce custom work that increases delivery cost.  
- Channel mix & CAC improvement: invest in channels with proven lower CAC and higher LTV; prioritize retention & upsell (cheaper than new customer acquisition).

Scalability (how to grow without proportionally increasing costs)
- Productize services: convert one‑off services into repeatable products or SaaS components to improve gross margins as scale grows.  
- Platform & APIs: enable self‑service and integrations so customers use fewer bespoke resources.  
- Monetize data/insights: package audience/insights as premium offerings with high margin.  
- Self‑service funnels & automation: shift onboarding, support, and reporting to self‑serve to reduce incremental cost per customer.  
- Partnerships & channel sales: use partners/resellers to expand reach without linear headcount growth.  
- Customer success & retention: increasing retention 5% can materially raise LTV and reduce churn-driven acquisition needs.

Key metrics to measure and show in the narrative
- Cash balance, monthly net burn, runway (months) — reconciled and scenarioed.  
- Gross margin and contribution margin by product line.  
- CAC, LTV, payback period.  
- Revenue growth rate (QoQ), ARPU or revenue per client, churn.  
- Operating leverage: change in operating expense as % of revenue over time.  
