1) Quick diagnostic (what to show in the deck)
- Key current numbers: cash, runway, monthly net burn, revenue run-rate (quarterly revenue × 4 / 4 for monthly), gross margin, contribution margin per product/service, top 10 customers % of revenue, AR and AP days.
- Cash runway sensitivity: show 3 scenarios (status quo, conservative cuts, growth + efficiency).
- P&L waterfall showing major drivers of profit change (revenue, gross margin, variable costs, fixed costs).
- KPIs: Gross margin %, Contribution margin, CAC, LTV, churn (if relevant), EBITDA, cash burn, months of runway.

2) Margin improvement levers (ranked by speed/impact)
- Revenue-side (often highest leverage in media businesses)
  - Push higher-margin products: prioritize retainer/managed services and productized offerings vs low-margin pass-through media buys.
  - Reprice where you undercharge (implement value-based pricing, yield-based ad pricing).
  - Upsell/cross-sell to existing clients (higher conversion cost, faster revenue).
  - Move more clients to recurring contracts (monthly retainers) to stabilize cash.
  - Introduce performance-fee components to capture upside.
- Cost-side (quick wins and structural)
  - Convert fixed costs to variable: hire contractors for campaign work, move software from perpetual to usage-based only where cheaper overall.
  - Renegotiate vendor and media supplier terms and payment timing.
  - Reduce discretionary spend (events, travel, noncritical tooling) for immediate runway lift.
  - Audit FTEs and agency relationships for duplication; freeze new hires except revenue-critical roles.
  - Improve media buying efficiency (programmatic optimization) to lower COGS per dollar of revenue.
- Operational / automation
  - Automate reporting and parts of campaign execution to reduce labor hours and errors.
  - Standardize scopes of work and templates to speed delivery and lower production cost.
- Balance sheet / working capital
  - Negotiate extended payment terms with suppliers; accelerate collections (shorter payment terms, early-pay discounts resisted unless necessary).
  - Offer clients small discounts for faster payments.
  - Consider short-term financing or a line of credit only if runway can't be recovered by cuts/growth.

3) Scalability levers (how to grow profitably)
- Productize services so gross margin scales: turn consultant work into packaged offerings (fixed price + variable uplift).
- Build platform or tooling (internal ops tools) to reduce marginal cost per campaign.
- Focus on net-new channels with higher ROI and scalable audiences.
- Standardize onboarding and playbooks to reduce time-to-value and CAC.
- Pursue partnerships and channel/white-label distribution to reach customers without proportional headcount growth.
- Measure and optimize CAC:LTV — ensure LTV >> CAC before scaling marketing spend.

4) Sample financial scenarios (illustrative — I’ll recalc once you confirm the burn/runway)
- Baseline: No change. Runway = cash / current burn.
- Efficiency scenario: 20% cut in fixed OpEx + 5% gross margin improvement from pricing/efficiency. Show new burn and runway.
- Growth + efficiency: 10% revenue increase (upsells + pricing) + 15% OpEx cut + 5% gross margin lift. Show break-even and runway uplift.

I can run the math and produce a 1-page memo or 5–7 slide internal deck that contains:
- Executive summary (1 slide): current cash, runway, ask (e.g., approve X savings & growth initiatives)
- Key metrics & sensitivity chart (1 slide)
- Top 5 levers with expected P&L impact (1 slide)
- 30/60/90-day plan with owners (1 slide)
- Risks & asks (hiring freezes, vendor concessions, small budget for growth experiments) (1 slide)

5) 30/60/90-day action plan (who does what)
- 0–30 days (quick wins)
  - Implement hiring freeze except revenue-critical roles.
  - Freeze discretionary spend and vendor re-negotiation kickoff.
  - Quick pricing review on top 10 clients; secure retainer conversions where possible.
  - Collections drive: offer 1–2% discount for 10–14 day payment.
- 30–60 days (operational changes)
  - Migrate 1–2 services to productized offerings.
  - Deploy automation for 1 high-effort process (report generation or campaign setup).
  - Implement new media yield KPIs and cost-per-outcome tracking.
- 60–90 days (scale & measure)
  - Launch targeted revenue experiments (pricing tiers or performance fees).
  - Review and lock in supplier payment terms.
  - Update forecasts and re-forecast runway.

6) Suggested finance narrative (short script you can use in a slide or memo)
- Current state: “Horizon Media entered the quarter with $1.2M cash and generated $1.8M revenue last quarter. We currently face a runway of X months at our present burn rate.”
- Thesis: “By prioritizing higher-margin products, converting clients to retainer models, and taking targeted cost actions, we can extend runway materially while keeping the organization positioned for growth.”
- Near-term ask: “Approve a 15% reduction in discretionary OpEx, authorize the pricing review for our top 10 clients, and empower the CFO to negotiate supplier terms. We will return in 60 days with a revised forecast showing runway improvement and revenue impact.”
- Benefits: “These actions protect core capability, extend runway, and improve gross and contribution margins so any future growth is profitable.”

7) Immediate next steps I can do for you
