- Reported: cash = $1.2M, runway = 6 months → implied burn = 1,200,000 / 6 = $200K/month.
Please confirm which is the correct monthly net cash burn (is $62K excluding some items, or is the 6-month runway using a different definition of runway?), or whether you mean gross burn vs net burn.

Key points to include in any finance narrative
- Current liquidity and runway (explicit monthly burn math).
- Recent revenue run-rate and growth trend (you gave $1.8M last quarter → ~ $600K/month run-rate).
- Primary drivers of burn (payroll, vendor spend, marketing/CAC, capex, one-time items).
- Near-term goal: extend runway to X months (specify target) and demonstrate path to profitability or next funding milestone.
- Concrete asks (cost-saves, hiring freeze, capital raise, pricing changes).

Executive (one-paragraph) finance narrative (ready for slide or email header)
Horizon Media has $1.2M of cash on hand. Based on current net burn, we must extend runway to reach our next milestone (target: 12 months) while preserving growth where it most efficiently improves unit economics. In the next 90 days we will deliver a prioritized set of actions focused on (1) high-impact cost reductions and renegotiations, (2) revenue / pricing optimization to improve gross margins, and (3) operational scalability (automation & tighter CAC controls). These actions are expected to reduce net burn, improve contribution margins, and buy time to execute strategic growth or financing options.

Priority margin-improvement levers (grouped & prioritized)
1) Revenue-side (higher ROI, often fastest to affect contribution margin)
 - Price increases where defensible (large customers / new contracts).
 - Product / service mix: push higher-margin offerings; sunset low-margin or loss-leader offerings.
 - Monetize existing assets: premium features, upsell/expansions, packaging changes.
 - Improve collection (shorten DSO), push prepayment or milestone billing.

2) Gross margin / COGS
 - Negotiate supplier/vendor contracts (volume discounts, extended payment terms).
 - Revisit fulfillment or delivery costs: outsourcing vs in-house.
 - Optimize variable input costs and automation to lower per-unit cost.

3) Operating expense reductions (OPEX)
 - Hiring freeze; prioritize critical roles only.
 - Contract reviews and cancellations of low-value subscriptions.
 - Temporary cuts to travel, events; shift to digital marketing with better measurable ROI.
 - Consider temporary salary reductions or deferred comp, with clear return-to-normal triggers.

4) Scalability & efficiency (medium term)
 - Invest in automation that reduces manual processing (billing, onboarding, reporting).
 - Improve sales efficiency: lower CAC by focusing on highest-LTV segments and better lead qualification.
 - Build customer success to increase retention and expansion (reduce churn).

5) Financing & structural moves
 - Short-term bridge financing or convertible note to extend runway if cuts would harm strategic growth.
 - Negotiate extended terms with landlords/lenders or get vendor payment plans.
 - Explore non-dilutive options (grants, tax credits).

90-day prioritized action plan (owner + expected impact)
- Week 0–2: Reconcile true burn (finance) and build a 3-scenario cash model (baseline, downside, stretch). Owner: Finance lead. Impact: immediate clarity on runway.
- Week 1–4: Hiring freeze + review all open reqs. Owner: HR + COO. Impact: ~10–30% reduction in OPEX (depends on hires).
- Week 1–6: Cut or renegotiate top 5 vendor contracts by spend. Owner: Procurement/Finance. Impact: one-off and recurring savings.
- Week 2–8: Launch pricing/packaging audit and 1–2 pilot price increases for strong segments. Owner: Product & Sales. Impact: 2–10% revenue uplift on pilot customers.
- Week 3–12: Sales focus: prioritize top channels with best CAC:LTV. Stop low-performing channels. Owner: Sales/Marketing. Impact: reduce CAC, increase ROI.
- Weeks 4–12: Implement 1–2 automation projects (billing, onboarding) that reduce headcount hours. Owner: Ops/IT. Impact: reduce variable operating cost over 6–12 months.
- Ongoing: Customer retention push to reduce churn intentionally. Owner: Customer Success. Impact: preserves revenue and LTV.

Scenario examples (useful to show in slides)
Assume monthly revenue = $600K (1.8M last quarter) — adjust based on your real run-rate.

If implied burn = $200K/month (current runway = 6 months):
- Baseline: 1.2M / 200k = 6 months.
- Reduce burn 25% → burn = 150k → runway = 1.2M / 150k = 8 months.
- Reduce burn 50% → burn = 100k → runway = 12 months.
- Improve net cash by increasing contribution margin (e.g., +10% on revenue) while keeping spend flat effectively lowers net burn.

If actual burn = $62K/month (current runway ≈ 19 months):
- Emphasize that runway is healthy, but measure burn components and re-evaluate growth investments that can be throttled vs accelerated.

Key metrics to include in slides/memo
- Cash on hand, monthly net cash burn, runway (explicit math).
- Revenue run-rate, QoQ growth.
- Gross margin % and contribution margin.
- CAC, LTV, CAC payback period.
- Monthly recurring revenue (MRR) if relevant, ARPU, churn.
- Operating expense breakdown (payroll % of revenue, marketing %, G&A).
- Scenario outputs (3-case model).

Suggested slide outline (5–7 slides)
1. Cover + One-line summary (cash, runway, ask)
2. Current financial snapshot (cash, burn, runway, revenue run-rate, margins)
3. Root-cause drivers of burn and margin pressure
4. 90-day plan with owners and expected savings / revenue uplift
5. 6–12 month scalability plan (automation, product changes, GTM)
6. Scenarios (baseline / downside / stretch) and KPIs
7. Ask: approvals needed (hiring freeze, vendor negotiation authority, pricing pilots, fundraising plan)

