Great — I can help. First, a couple of quick clarifying items, then a set of practical margin-improvement and scalability levers, short financial scenarios (so you can use numbers in the narrative), suggested KPIs, and a tidy finance narrative you can drop into internal materials.

1) Clarify one inconsistency
- You gave: Cash = $1.2M, runway = 6 months, burn = $62K/month.
- Check: runway = cash / burn. If burn = $62K/mo, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months, not 6 months.
- If runway is actually 6 months, implied burn = 1.2M / 6 = $200K/month.
Please confirm which is correct (is burn $62K or ~$200K?), and whether “burn” you gave is net burn (post-revenue) or operating cash outflow (pre-revenue). Also confirm whether revenue $1.8M last quarter is recognized revenue (not bookings) and whether it’s representative going forward.

2) Key baseline calculations (using your numbers)
- Revenue last quarter = $1.8M → monthly revenue ≈ $600K.
- If burn = $62K/mo:
  - Runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months.
  - Net monthly cash flow ≈ 600K revenue - 62K burn = +538K (this implies strong monthly positive cash generation — check definitions).
- If runway = 6 months (implied burn ≈ $200K/mo):
  - Runway = 1.2M / 200K = 6 months (consistent).
  - Net monthly cash flow ≈ 600K revenue - 200K burn = +400K (still positive; again check definitions).

Note: These quick checks show there’s likely a definition mismatch: “burn” normally refers to net cash outflow after revenues (or sometimes operating cash burn excluding financing). Please confirm the expense breakdown.

3) Immediate, high-impact margin improvement levers (with rationale)
- Increase pricing and packaging (1–6 months)
  - Re-price top 20% of clients where value is highest or add premium tiers. A 5–10% price increase on higher-value contracts can deliver immediate margin lift with low incremental cost.
- Shift revenue mix to higher-margin products/services (3–12 months)
  - Focus sales on offerings with highest gross margin; sunset low-margin engagements or convert them to fixed-fee or subscription models.
- Improve gross margin (COGS) via supplier/vendor renegotiation and automation (1–6 months)
  - Renegotiate contracts, consolidate vendors, or move parts of delivery to tooling/automation. Target 3–8% reduction in COGS initially.
- Reduce operating expenses / optimize headcount (30–90 days)
  - Identify non-core roles, freeze non-critical hires, consolidate tools. A disciplined 10–20% OPEX cut can materially extend runway with limited revenue impact.
- Operational efficiency & process standardization (3–9 months)
  - Standardize deliverables, create templates, and reduce bespoke work. Lowers delivery cost per engagement and scales revenue.
- Move to recurring revenue (6–18 months)
  - Convert services to subscription products, retainer models, or platform usage fees. Recurring revenue increases predictability and valuation multiples.
- Increase sales productivity and focus on channel partnerships (3–12 months)
  - Implement/expand channel partners, referral programs, and variable comp to scale revenue without linear headcount increases.
- Improve billing & working capital (0–3 months)
  - Shorten payment terms, tighten collections, or use invoice financing to improve cash conversion and reduce net working capital needs.

4) Example scenario impacts (illustrative)
Assume current monthly revenue = $600K.

Scenario A — Moderate cuts (target: 15% OPEX reduction)
- If implied burn = $200K → reduce OPEX 15% → new burn = 170K
- New runway = 1.2M / 170K ≈ 7.1 months (+1.1 months)
- Annualized OPEX savings ≈ 30K/mo * 12 = $360K

Scenario B — Mix + pricing lift
- Apply 5% price increase on 60% of revenue (high-value accounts): incremental revenue ≈ 0.05 * 0.6 * 600K = $18K/mo
- Combine with 5% COGS reduction (if COGS were 50% of revenue, initial COGS=300K; 5% reduction = 15K/mo)
- Combined immediate monthly margin improvement ≈ $33K → annualized ≈ $396K

Scenario C — Aggressive efficiency (25% OPEX reduction + 5% price lift)
- If burn = $200K and OPEX is driving most of that, 25% OPEX cut + price lift could reduce burn to ~140–150K and add ~20K revenue monthly.
- Runway then ≈ 1.2M / 150K = 8 months — plus improved profitability and optional reinvestment in growth.

I can build a 12-month P&L/cash model with these scenarios once you confirm the true burn and give a quick expense split (COGS, fixed OPEX, variable OPEX).

5) Scalability levers (how to grow without linear cost)
- Productize and bundle services into repeatable packages.
- Invest in tooling and automation to reduce delivery time per unit.
- Hire a small BizDev/Partnership role to open channel revenue that scales by partner sales.
- Standardize onboarding and onboarding docs to reduce CSM load and enable higher client-to-CX ratios.
- Adopt usage-based or subscription pricing to lock in recurring revenue and increase LTV.

6) Metrics / KPIs to include in the finance narrative
- Cash runway (months)
- Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR) or average monthly recognized revenue
- Gross margin % (revenue - COGS) / revenue
- Net burn (cash outflow after operating receipts)
- Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) and CAC payback
- Lifetime Value (LTV) and LTV:CAC ratio
- Gross churn & net revenue retention
- Contribution margin per product line or client cohort

7) Suggested concise finance narrative (internal slide / email language)
- Current position: “As of [date], Horizon Media has $1.2M in cash and recognized $1.8M in revenue last quarter (≈ $600K/month). Runway is currently X months based on current burn of $Y/month.” (Fill X/Y after confirming.)
- Key insight: “We have clear levers to materially improve margins and extend runway while preserving growth: pricing, productization of high-margin offerings, COGS reductions, and disciplined OPEX management.”
- Proposed plan (90-day focus):
  1. Validate financial baseline and reconcile burn/runway (Immediate).
  2. Implement targeted 5–10% price increases on top 20% of clients + 30-day communications plan (30–60 days).
  3. Identify and implement 10–20% OPEX reductions (30–60 days) focused on non-customer-facing and duplicative spend.
  4. Launch productization initiative for two scalable offerings (60–120 days).
  5. Deliver a 12-month forecast with three scenarios (base / efficiency / growth) for the leadership review (45–60 days).
- Ask / calls-to-action: “Approve a 60-day task force (Finance + Head of Sales + Head of Ops) to implement pricing and cost actions; allow up to $X for automation tools if required. Deliverable: updated 12-month cash model and slide deck for the board.”

8) Immediate next steps I can take for you
- I can build a slide-ready 1-page financial summary and a 12-month P&L/cash forecast with 3 scenarios — I’ll need:
  - Confirmed burn (is it $62K or $200K?), and whether burn is net or gross.
  - P&L split last quarter or monthly (Revenue, COGS, Sales & Marketing, G&A, R&D/Ops).
  - Any known upcoming one-time cash items (leases, legal, payments).
  - Customer mix by revenue concentration (top 10 clients % of revenue).
- Or I can draft the internal finance narrative email/slide text for leadership based on the assumptions above.

Which of the next steps would you like me to do? If you want the forecast/materials, please confirm the correct burn/runway number and paste a quick expense split (even approximate).

