I can help. First a quick check on the numbers, then concrete margin-improvement and scalability levers, a 30/60/90-day action plan, the key KPIs/metrics to include in your finance narrative, a short executive finance narrative you can use, and a few slide/email bullets you can drop into a deck or update.

Clarify one thing
- You said: cash $1.2M, burn $62K/month, runway 6 months. Those three numbers don’t line up: 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months of runway (not 6). If the 6-month runway is correct, the implied burn is ~ $200K/month. Which is right: is burn $62K/month today, or is there some planned/seasonal spend, capex, or debt service that raises real cash outflow to ~200K/month? I’ll proceed assuming the $62K/month burn and $1.2M cash are accurate but tell me if the 6-month figure is the binding constraint.

Quick summary math (if $62K burn/month)
- Cash: $1.2M
- Monthly burn: $62K
- Runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months
- Last quarter revenue: $1.8M → Annualize = $7.2M run-rate

If runway truly is 6 months, tell me the missing outflows and I’ll re-run everything.

High-level goals for the narrative
- Show runway and liquidity transparently
- Demonstrate strong revenue traction (quarterly revenue → ARR)
- Show concrete margin improvement levers and timelines
- Present unit economics and scalable operating model
- Ask for any decisions/resources needed (hiring, investment, pricing changes)

Concrete margin-improvement levers
Short-term (0–90 days, high impact / low friction)
- Reduce discretionary operating spend: freeze non-essential travel, marketing pilots, consulting. Target 10–20% reduction immediately.
- Hiring freeze and careful contractor review. Pause open requisitions; renegotiate contractor rates.
- Renegotiate vendor contracts / payment terms: push longer payment terms and lower rates where possible.
- Tighten receivables: invoice immediately, shorten payment terms, incentivize early pay, pursue AR collections to improve cash inflows.
- Trim low-margin offerings or unprofitable customer segments.
- Temporary reduction of non-critical subscriptions/saas and consolidated licenses.

Medium-term (90–180 days)
- Price optimization: raise prices where market allows, introduce tiered pricing or value-added bundles to increase average revenue per customer.
- Product re-margining: shift sales toward higher-margin services/features and sunset low-margin lines.
- Process automation: reduce manual operations that scale poorly (billing, onboarding, customer success workflows).
- Outsource non-core functions (payroll, some ops) where it lowers cost without hurting quality.
- Vendor consolidation and volume negotiation once you have consolidated demand.

Strategic / long-term (6–18 months)
- Move from bespoke, manual services to repeatable productized offerings that scale (SaaS features, packages).
- Monetize platform/tech: APIs, add-on services, marketplace.
- Improve unit economics: lower CAC via better inbound funnels, increase LTV through retention, upsell and cross-sell playbooks.
- Invest in predictive analytics for churn and cohort margin analysis to prioritize top accounts.

Scalability levers (operational & GTM)
- Standardize onboarding and playbooks so each new customer costs less to onboard (lower COGS/customer).
- Invest in automation and self-service tooling for customers (reduces support cost per account).
- KPI-driven customer success (identify expansion accounts early).
- Build scalable sales motions: inside sales + outbound + channel partners, rather than only high-touch enterprise sales.
- Modularize product so new customers can be configured rather than built.

30/60/90-day action plan (practical)
- Days 0–30:
  - Reconcile runway and create a 13-week cash forecast (weekly).
  - Identify top 10 cost lines that can be trimmed immediately; implement hiring freeze.
  - Start vendor renegotiations and tighten AR collections.
  - Run break-even and contribution-margin analysis by product.
- Days 31–60:
  - Implement pricing tests / increase on a pilot segment.
  - Launch cost-savings programs (consolidate SaaS, cut non-essential spend).
  - Build unit-economics model: CAC, LTV, payback period by channel.
- Days 61–90:
  - Automate 1–2 high-cost operational processes (billing/onboarding).
  - Present updated forecast and scenario analysis to leadership/investors with a list of required decisions (e.g., funding, headcount).
  - Start roll-out of scaled GTM motions for higher-margin products.

Key metrics to include in the finance narrative
- Cash on hand and runway (explicit math and scenarios)
- Monthly Net Cash Burn (and gross burn & cash inflows)
- ARR / Quarterly revenue and YoY or QoQ growth
- Gross margin % (by product if possible)
- Contribution margin per customer / cohort
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio, CAC payback months
- Churn (gross & net revenue retention)
- Day Sales Outstanding (DSO), Days Payable Outstanding (DPO)
- Customer concentration (top 10 customers % of revenue)
- Operating expense breakdown (people, marketing, product, G&A)

Simple scenario examples (illustrative)
- Baseline: burn = $62K → runway ≈ 19.4 months
- Cost-cut scenario: cut burn 20% → burn ≈ $49.6K → runway ≈ 24.2 months
- Conservative runway (if burn ~ $200K as implied by 6 months): runway = 6 months — immediate priority to cut costs or raise cash

Suggested short finance narrative (one paragraph)
"Horizon Media finished last quarter with $1.8M in revenue (annualized run-rate $7.2M) and $1.2M in cash. Our current net cash burn is $62K/month (providing ~19 months of runway at current run-rate); we are running a disciplined cost and growth program to accelerate margin improvement. Over the next 90 days we will implement immediate expense reductions, accelerate collections, renegotiate vendor contracts, and pilot targeted price increases—measures expected to improve margins and extend runway materially. Simultaneously we will invest selectively in product and automation to move the business to a more scalable, higher-margin model. We will present a 13-week cash forecast, unit-economics dashboard (CAC/LTV/payback), and scenario plans to the board within 30 days and request decisions on any required funding or headcount trade-offs."

Slide / deck bullets (3–4 slides)
- Slide 1 — Current snapshot: cash, burn, runway (explicit math), last quarter revenue & ARR, gross margin (if available)
- Slide 2 — Immediate actions (0–90 days): AR push, hiring freeze, vendor renegotiation, pricing tests, top cost cuts
- Slide 3 — Medium-term margin & scalability roadmap: productize, automated onboarding, pricing tiers, retention playbook
- Slide 4 — KPIs + scenarios: baseline vs cost-cut vs growth scenarios with impact on runway; ask/decision points

Requests I need from you to make this concrete
- Confirm whether burn $62K/month is net cash burn today or some other measure
- Gross margin % and a top-line operating expense breakdown (people, marketing, product, G&A)
- Customer concentration (top 5 or 10 customers % of revenue) and churn/retention if available
- Your priority: extend runway, grow revenue fast, prepare for fundraising, or sell/exit?

Next steps I can do for you
- Draft the 13-week cash-forecast and scenario table (I can prepare a template) — I’ll need your expense breakdown and AR schedule.
- Create the one-page finance memo or the 4-slide deck bullets and speaker notes.
- Draft the email/board memo to explain the situation and request decisions or funding.

Which of the above would you like me to prepare next? If you want the cash-forecast/scenario work, please upload or paste your latest P&L / expense schedule (monthly), AR aging, and any near-term committed spend.

