Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Before I draft the finance narrative and specific materials, one quick clarification so my recommendations and any scenario modeling are accurate:

- The numbers you gave look inconsistent. Runway is normally cash divided by net burn. With $1.2M cash and a burn of $62K/month, runway = 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.3 months. If runway is actually 6 months, that implies a net burn of about $200K/month (1,200,000 / 6). Which of these is correct? Or are you using a different definition of “burn” or “runway” (e.g., burn = operating expenses excluding revenue, runway measured after factoring in forecasted revenue)?  

If you want, tell me which number to trust (cash + burn or stated runway), or paste a quick monthly P&L (revenue, COGS, fixed OpEx, variable OpEx) and I’ll model scenarios precisely.

Meanwhile, here are concrete, ready-to-use recommendations and a suggested finance narrative you can use immediately.

Key derived metrics (from the numbers you gave)
- Quarter revenue: $1.8M → monthly average revenue ≈ $600K → annualized run-rate ≈ $7.2M.
- Runway (two interpretations):
