- Also revenue last quarter $1.8M → average monthly revenue that quarter = $600K.

Which of these is correct:
A) Net cash burn is $62K/month (so runway ≈ 19 months) or
B) Runway is actually 6 months (so net burn ≈ $200K/month)?
Or do you use a different definition of “burn” (e.g., operating cash burn vs. gross payroll/opex)?

Tell me which is right (or I can show both scenarios). Below I provide immediate analysis, prioritized levers, sample calculations, and a concise finance narrative you can use in internal materials.

1) Quick scenario math (using standard definition: burn = net cash outflow per month = expenses – cash receipts)
- Revenue per month (last quarter): $1.8M / 3 = $600K/month.
Scenario A — burn = $62K/mo:
  - Runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months (healthy).
  - Implied total monthly expenses = revenue + burn = 600K + 62K = $662K.
Scenario B — runway = 6 months (implied burn = $200K/mo):
  - Implied net burn = 200K/month.
  - Implied total monthly expenses = 600K + 200K = $800K.
Implication: confirm which is accurate before final recommendations or investor-ready slides.

2) Priority margin-improvement & scalability levers (grouped and ranked)
A. Revenue / price mix (fast, high ROI)
  - Raise pricing / introduce premium tiers (even +3–5% increases across services can be high-impact).
  - Focus on upsell/cross-sell to existing customers (short sales cycle, higher margin).
  - Reallocate sales from low-margin to high-margin products/channels.
  - Launch quick pilots for add-on services (data/analytics, creative premium placement).

B. Gross margin / cost of goods sold
  - Negotiate vendor/partner fees (platform fees, media buying rebates).
  - Improve media buying efficiency (programmatic optimization, better targeting).
  - Shift spend toward channels with higher return, reduce low-ROI campaigns.

C. Operating expense (OPEX) / structural cost
  - Hiring freeze for non-critical roles; prioritize revenue-generating hires.
  - Reduce discretionary spend (events, travel, software consolidation).
  - Automate manual workflows (billing, reporting) to reduce headcount pressure.
  - Outsource or renegotiate agency/consultant contracts.

D. Cash & working capital
  - Re-negotiate payment terms with suppliers (extend payables), accelerate customer collections (shorten receivables).
  - Pause or defer non-essential CAPEX.

E. Strategic scalability (medium-term)
  - Productize services (repeatable packages) to improve unit economics.
  - Invest in tech/infrastructure that yields lower marginal cost per additional client.
  - Track CAC vs LTV and raise spend where CAC payback is <12 months.

3) Example impact estimates (illustrative)
Note: substitute with your actual figures when available.
- 5% price increase on $600K/mo revenue => +$30K/month incremental revenue.
- 10% reduction in non-personnel discretionary opex (assume $100K/month base) => save $10K/month.
- Vendor renegotiation saving => $15–30K/month.
- Hiring freeze (defer 2 hires at $8K/month each) => save $16K/month.
Combined small moves could easily add $70–100K/month in margin improvement — enough to convert an implied $200K burn toward breakeven or materially extend runway.

4) What to monitor — essential KPIs for the narrative
- Net burn ($/month) and runway (months)
- Revenue run-rate and QoQ growth
- Gross margin % and contribution margin per product
- CAC, LTV, CAC payback period
- ARPU / revenue per client
- Customer churn and retention rates
- Receivables days & payables days
- Operating expense by function (sales, R&D, G&A)

5) Recommended 30/60/90 plan (practical, prioritized)
30 days
  - Confirm and reconcile cash, burn, and runway figures.
  - Freeze non-essential hiring and discretionary spend.
  - Quick vendor renegotiation outreach for immediate savings.
  - Run scenario modeling for 3 runway cases (baseline, downside, best-case).
60 days
  - Implement pricing/packaging pilots with top clients.
  - Deploy targeted cost-cutting (software consolidation, renegotiated media fees).
  - Set weekly cash cadence with CFO/ops for burn tracking.
90 days
  - Launch productization or automation initiatives for scalability.
  - Re-assess hiring plan and reallocate budget to growth initiatives that show positive unit economics.

6) Suggested concise finance narrative (two versions — short and slightly expanded)

Short:
"Horizon Media finished last quarter with $1.2M in cash and revenue of $1.8M (avg $600K/month). We are focused on extending runway and improving margins through three immediate priorities: (1) pricing and revenue mix improvements to capture higher-margin revenue, (2) targeted cost reductions in vendor and discretionary spend, and (3) operational changes to improve working capital. These actions, combined with a 30/60/90 execution plan, should materially extend runway and improve unit economics while protecting growth investments."

Expanded (for a slide or email to leadership):
"Current state: $1.2M cash, $600K average monthly revenue. Objective: materially extend runway and increase gross and contribution margins while sustaining high-return growth initiatives. We will pursue a three-part plan: (A) Revenue optimization — immediate pricing tests, upsells, and reallocation toward higher-margin channels; (B) Margin capture — renegotiate vendor contracts, improve media buy efficiency, and consolidate low-value spend; (C) Structural scaling — freeze non-critical hires, automate back-office ops, and productize repeatable services. We will measure progress with weekly burn reporting, monthly margin reviews, and KPIs (CAC/LTV, gross margin, runway). Requested leadership approvals: temporary hiring freeze, authority to negotiate vendor terms, and go/no-go thresholds for pricing pilots."

7) Next steps I can do for you
- If you confirm which burn/runway number is correct, I can build a 3-scenario financial model (monthly P&L and runway) and show the exact impact of different saves or revenue levers.
