Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick note before I dive in: two of the inputs you gave conflict. You said cash = $1.2M and runway = 6 months, but you also said burn = $62K/month. Runway = cash / net burn, so:
- If burn = $62K/month, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months.
- If runway = 6 months, net burn must be ≈ $200K/month (1.2M / 6).

Which of those is correct (burn = $62K or runway = 6 months)? Also: is the $1.8M revenue figure for the last quarter (i.e., ~600K/month)? Are there one-time items, upcoming large cash outflows, or receivables/payables that affect runway? Once you confirm, I’ll build a short model and recommended slides. Below I give recommendations and a finance narrative that work for both “urgent runway” and “scale/improve margins” cases.

Top-line summary / approach
- Clarify the true net burn and any one-off cash items (see question above).
- Run a 13-week cash forecast immediately to identify true short-term outflows.
- Two parallel tracks: (A) immediate cash preservation if runway is tight, (B) margin & scalability improvements to increase profitability and lift LTV/CAC long-term.

Immediate (0–30 days) — stop the bleeding if runway is short
1. 13-week cashflow and scenario plan (highest priority). Identify exact weekly inflows/outflows.
2. Freeze discretionary spend and hiring; defer non-critical CapEx.
3. AR and working capital:
   - Accelerate receivables: offer 1–2% discount for 7–14 day prepayments, push collections on big invoices.
   - Negotiate extended payment terms with key vendors.
4. Vendor & contract renegotiations: ask for reduced rates, extended payment schedules, or temporary pauses.
5. Pause low-ROI marketing and reallocate toward highest-converting activities.

Estimated impact (example if runway = 6 months / burn = $200K):
- Reduce OPEX by $30–80K/month via freeze and small cuts.
- Vendor negotiation & payment-term changes may free another $20–50K/month.
- AR acceleration could provide immediate working capital (variable).
- Combined, realistic near-term reduction could turn $200K burn → $70–120K burn, extending runway substantially.

Near-term (1–3 months) — quick revenue & margin wins
1. Pricing & packaging: small targeted price increases (2–5%) on non-sensitive products or add-value bundling.
   - If monthly revenue = $600K, a 2% price rise = +$12K/month.
2. Upsell / cross-sell campaigns to existing customers — highest ROI revenue.
3. Cut or rework low-margin offerings; prioritize high-margin services/products.
4. Marketing efficiency: reallocate spend to channels with best CAC payback.
5. Launch prepaid/annual plans with discounts to improve cash collection and retention.

Medium-term (3–12 months) — structural margin & scalability moves
1. Productize & automate:
   - Move bespoke work toward productized offerings to increase gross margin and reduce delivery cost per client.
2. Move fixed costs to variable costs where possible (outsourcing, partner models).
3. Invest in self-serve onboarding / tooling to lower support/CS costs per customer as you grow.
4. Sales process standardization and channel partnerships to scale revenue without linear sales headcount growth.
5. Procurement & supply chain optimization to reduce COGS.

Estimated structural impact (example assumptions)
- Raise gross margin by 3–5 p.p. on an annual run-rate of $7.2M (last quarter annualized):
  - 5 p.p. margin improvement = 0.05 * 7.2M = $360K/year ≈ $30K/month.
- Reduce monthly OPEX growth by automations/outsourcing: save $20–50K/month over 6–12 months.
- These together can materially reduce net burn and improve runway and profitability.

KPIs to monitor weekly/monthly
- Cash runway (weeks) and 13-week cash forecast
- Net burn (cash outflows – inflows) monthly
- Gross margin % and contribution margin per customer
- MRR (or monthly revenue), revenue growth rate
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio, CAC payback period
- Churn rate, AR (DSO), and deferred revenue / prepayments

Prioritized 4–point action plan to present to leadership / board
1. Run 13-week cash forecast and confirm net burn (immediate).
2. Implement a 30-day cost and hiring freeze + vendor/payables negotiation.
3. Launch top-3 quick revenue levers (price tweaks, prepaid discounts, targeted upsells).
4. Develop a 6–12 month margin plan: productize offerings, automate delivery, and improve procurement.

Sample finance narrative (short — use in an investor/board slide)
- Current state: “Horizon Media holds $1.2M in cash. Last quarter revenue was $1.8M (≈ $600K/month). Our current net burn is [confirm figure], giving us an estimated runway of [confirm months].”
- Key risk: “If net burn remains at current levels, runway will be constrained within [X] months. This increases financial risk and limits our ability to invest in scalable growth.”
- Plan & milestones: “Over the next 90 days we will (1) execute a 13-week cash forecast and freeze discretionary spend; (2) secure immediate cash via AR acceleration and vendor negotiations; (3) test pricing and upsell campaigns to increase near-term cash inflows. Over 3–12 months we will productize services and automate delivery to improve gross margins by 3–5 p.p. and reduce OPEX growth.”
- Ask / outcome: “With these actions we expect to extend runway to >12 months and improve monthly net cash generation by $X–$Y within 6 months, enabling selective growth investments backed by stronger unit economics.”

What I can deliver next
- A one-page finance memo for the exec team (with the numbers filled in using the correct burn/runway assumption).
- A 3-slide investor/board-ready deck (current state, 90-day plan, 6–12 month roadmap + KPIs).
- A short 13-week cash flow model and two scenarios (base / downside) if you confirm whether burn = $62K or runway = 6 months and whether $1.8M is quarter revenue.

Tell me:
1) Which is correct: net burn = $62K/month, or runway = 6 months (implying burn ≈ $200K/month)?
2) Do you want a one-pager, 3-slide deck, or the 13-week cash model next? If so, I’ll prepare it and include the scenario analysis and recommended slide text.

