Assuming you want practical, prioritized actions and a finance narrative for internal materials, below is a compact plan: reconciliation, quick wins to improve margin, medium/long-term scalability levers, the financial narrative outline and recommended metrics/slides. If you confirm the numbers I’ll build the model and write the memo/slide copy.

1) Reconcile & immediate diagnostics (what I’ll build if you want)
- Verify definitions: cash balance, runway basis (fixed monthly cash burn or runway-targeted cash reserve), burn definition (gross vs net), revenue recognition (net vs pass-through media).
- Produce a 12-month cash flow projection with 3 scenarios (base / downside / upside) and show runway under each.
- Show contribution margin and operating margin by product/service line (if you have breakdowns).

2) Quick high-impact margin-improvement moves (0–3 months)
- Remove non-essential fixed costs: freeze hiring, pause non-critical subscriptions, renegotiate leases and vendor contracts. These are immediate, low-friction savings.
- Reprice underperforming offerings: raise price on bespoke/white-glove services where value is high and price elasticity is low.
- Reduce pass-through / vendor margin leakage: move to direct supplier relationships where possible or re-negotiate media/vendor fees.
- Improve billing / collections: tighten payment terms, offer small discounts for faster payment, reduce DSO.
- Tighten project profitability controls: require signoffs for scope creep, enforce change-order billing, and use standard templates to capture all billable time.

3) Operational / structural levers for sustained margin improvement (3–12 months)
- Productize services: standardize service packages with clear pricing and deliverable templates to increase utilization and reduce delivery costs.
- Automation and tooling: automate reporting, onboarding, and recurring delivery tasks to lower variable delivery cost per account.
- Shift to higher-margin offers: subscription/retainer models, data or insight products, and platform services (recurring revenue).
- Optimize go-to-market: focus sales on higher LTV customers, reduce expensive channel acquisition, improve cross-sell/upsell.
- Centralize shared services: consolidate finance, HR, procurement to reduce duplicative overhead as you scale.
- Optimize headcount mix: increase seniority in sales/CS that drive revenue; offload commodity work to lower-cost regions/outsourcers or contractors.

4) Scalability tactics
- Standardize onboarding and playbooks so new accounts scale without proportional staff increases.
- Build self-service or semi-self-service products (dashboards, reporting) to reduce recurring manual effort.
- KPI-driven elasticity: build customer segmentation by margin and prioritize growth where gross margin and LTV:CAC are highest.
- Invest in platform/technology where payback < 12–18 months to increase throughput per employee.
- Partnerships/marketplaces: white-label or embed services into partner platforms to acquire customers at lower incremental cost.

5) Financial narrative structure (what to present to leadership / board)
- Executive summary (1 slide/paragraph): current cash, runway, revenue trend, ask (e.g., “We propose to improve margin by X% and extend runway to Y months; approval to execute A-B-C.”)
- Current state (1–2 slides): P&L last 3 quarters, cash position, burn and runway details, top 5 cost drivers.
- Key issues & opportunities (1 slide): where margin is leaking (pass-throughs, manual delivery, underpriced services).
- Proposed levers & timeline (2–3 slides): prioritized actions (quick wins vs structural investments), expected savings or revenue upside, owners and timeline.
- Financial scenarios (2 slides): base/downside/upside P&Ls and cash, breakeven analysis, sensitivities to revenue and cost.
- Ask & KPI dashboard (1 slide): resources or approvals requested, 90/180/365 day milestones, KPIs to report (see list below).
- Risks & mitigations (1 slide).

6) KPIs and metrics to include
- Revenue: monthly revenue, revenue growth rate, revenue by product line.
- Gross margin (and gross margin by product).
- Contribution margin per account and per service.
- EBITDA / Operating margin.
- Burn rate (net cash burn) and runway (cash / net burn).
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC.
- AR, DSO, and churn (if recurring revenue).
- Utilization (%) and revenue per FTE.
- Customer cohort economics (3/6/12 month).

