Length: 7073 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First, a quick clarification I need before I prepare any slides or models: the numbers you gave are inconsistent.

- Cash = $1.2M and runway = 6 months implies a net burn of ~ $200k/month (1.2M ÷ 6).
- You also wrote burn = $62k/month, which with $1.2M cash implies ~19.4 months of runway (1.2M ÷ 62k).
Which of these is correct (runway or burn)? Also: is the $1.8M revenue last quarter recurring / steady monthly run rate ($600k/month) or lumpy? Once you confirm I’ll build a short model and slides. Meanwhile, below are immediate recommendations, a prioritized action plan, margin & scalability levers, key metrics to include in the finance narrative, and a sample one‑page narrative you can use with the board.

Immediate priorities (first 0–30 days)
- Reconcile cash and commitments: verify bank balance, committed but unpaid liabilities, and any timing-sensitive vendor or payroll dates. Short-term cash visibility is critical.
- Stop non-essential spend: institute a hiring freeze, pause non-ROI marketing initiatives, and delay discretionary projects until we model scenarios.
- Accelerate cash in: push AR collections (early-pay discounts, tighten DSO), accelerate invoicing, and capture any outstanding receivables.
- Stretch cash out: renegotiate vendor payment terms (30→60/90 days where possible), defer capital expenditures, and review lease/contract termination costs.
- Communicate: produce a one-page update to the CEO/Board with current cash, runway, ask, and 90-day plan to buy time/approval for changes.

Concrete margin-improvement levers
1) Revenue / pricing
  - Reprice high-volume products to increase contribution margin; test tiered pricing for premium features.
  - Shift sales focus to higher-margin customer segments and upsells (bundle services with higher margins).
  - Shorten sales cycles for high-value contracts through incentives (fast-start discounts that revert).
2) Gross margin / COGS
  - Re-negotiate supplier contracts and get volume discounts or extended payment terms.
  - Identify and eliminate low-margin SKUs/services.
  - Move more work to variable cost models (contractors, outsourcing) instead of fixed headcount where feasible.
3) Operating expenses (Opex)
  - Reduce discretionary SaaS, vendor subscriptions; turn off idle cloud resources and optimize cloud spend.
  - Consolidate tools, eliminate duplicate services, renegotiate vendor pricing.
  - Re-examine office footprint and real estate expenses (hybrid policy changes).
4) People costs
  - Hiring freeze + reassign internal resources to revenue-critical work.
  - Use limited, targeted hiring for revenue-driving roles; consider temporary contractors.
  - Implement compensation deferrals or short-term bonus incentives tied to revenue milestones if necessary.
5) One-time actions
  - Sell non-core assets, accelerate deferred revenue where legal, and pursue bridge financing (convertible note, line of credit) if runway is short.

Scalability levers (longer-term, 3–12 months)
- Productize offerings to reduce customized delivery time and increase gross margins.
- Invest in automation to reduce per-customer servicing cost (onboarding, support workflows).
- Standardize packages/pricing to reduce sales cycle and increase upsell efficiency.
- Build channel/partnerships to scale distribution without proportional headcount growth.
- Measure unit economics (LTV:CAC) and ensure customer acquisition focuses on positive unit economics.

Key metrics to include in the finance narrative
- Cash on hand, net burn (cash out – cash in), runway (months)
- Monthly revenue (and QoQ growth), gross margin %, contribution margin per customer
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio, payback period
- DSO (days sales outstanding), AR aging
- Fixed vs. variable cost split, headcount by function and % of expense
- Churn (if subscription), ARR / MRR if applicable
- Scenario projections: base, cost-cut, and revenue-acceleration cases

Sample one-page finance narrative (use as the opening slide / email)
- Current position: "As of [date] Horizon Media holds $1.2M cash. Last quarter revenue was $1.8M. Based on current net burn of [$X]/month, runway is Y months. We are taking immediate actions to extend runway and improve margins."
- Root causes: "Cash pressure is driven by [list: higher fixed operating costs / investments / slower collections / short-term capital commitments]. Our product mix yields variable gross margins across segments."
- Plan & impact (90 days): "1) Cost reduction & cash preservation (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiation) — target reduce net burn by 25% within 60 days. 2) Revenue acceleration (focus on top 20% highest margin clients and upsell program) — target +10% revenue in 90 days from prioritized deals. 3) Finance moves: tighten AR, extend AP, explore a $X bridge facility if needed." 
- Ask / decision requested: "Approval to implement hiring freeze and vendor payment-term negotiations, and authorization to engage short-term bridge financing if runway < 6 months after immediate actions."
- Milestones & owner: "Weekly cash updates to CEO & Board; 30/60/90 day checkpoints with named owners for collections, cost, and revenue initiatives."

90-day action plan (prioritized, with owners and targets)
- Week 0–2: Verify cash and commitments; issue one-page to Board. Owner: CFO. Target: accurate cash-by-week view.
- Week 1–4: Hiring freeze; identify >10% discretionary Opex to cut. Owner: Head of Ops. Target: immediate 10–20% reduction in discretionary spend.
- Week 2–6: AR push + payment incentives; renegotiate critical vendor terms. Owner: Head of RevOps. Target: reduce DSO by X days, unlock $Y in cash.
- Week 3–8: Focus sales on top-margin accounts; implement upsell campaigns. Owner: Head of Sales. Target: add $Z revenue/month and improve blended gross margin by N points.
- Week 4–12: Implement cloud and SaaS optimization; reduce recurring vendor costs. Owner: CTO/CFO. Target: reduce monthly fixed costs by $Q.

Simple scenario examples (illustrative)
- Scenario A (use your stated burn = $62k): runway = 1.2M ÷ 62k ≈ 19.4 months. If we reduce burn 30% → new burn ≈ $43k → runway ≈ 28 months.
- Scenario B (runway = 6 months is correct): implied burn ≈ $200k/month. To reach 12 months we must reduce burn to $100k/month or increase cash / financing by ~$1.2M.
I’ll build accurate scenarios once you confirm which numbers are correct and share monthly P&L or at least monthly revenue and expense breakdown.

What I can do next for you
- Draft a crisp 6-slide board deck (current state, runway, top 5 initiatives + impact, 90-day plan, scenarios, ask).
- Build a three-scenario financial model (base / cost-cut / revenue-growth) and a 12-month cash-flow table.
- Draft the Board/CFO email summarizing the ask and recommended approvals.

Tell me:
1) Which of the inconsistent numbers is correct (burn or runway)? Or share monthly P&L / cash-flow for the last 3 months.
2) Do you want the one-page board update, a slide deck, or the financial model first? If slides/model, do you prefer Google Sheets / Excel or simple tables in this chat?

