Thanks — I can help. First, one important clarification before I build numbers or slides:

- Your numbers are inconsistent as given. With $1.2M cash and a $62K/month burn, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months, not 6 months. If runway is actually 6 months, the implied burn is ~ $200K/month (1.2M / 6). Also, revenue last quarter $1.8M implies ~ $600K/month revenue — is that recognized revenue, cash collections, or bookings? Are these top-line numbers gross or net of COGS? Are you reporting burn as net cash burn (cash outflows minus inflows) or operating expense run-rate?

Please confirm:
1. Which of the following is correct (or explain differences): runway = 6 months OR burn = $62K/month?  
2. Is the $1.8M last-quarter revenue cash collected? recurring? one-off/project based?
3. Do you have a rough split of fixed vs variable monthly costs, and gross margin (or COGS)?

While you confirm, here’s a focused, actionable plan and a draft finance narrative you can use immediately. I’ll mark assumptions where I had to infer.

Executive summary (short)
- Current position (assumption): Cash = $1.2M; last quarter revenue = $1.8M (≈ $600K/month). Reported burn = $62K/month (if accurate), giving ~19 months runway. If runway is actually 6 months, then monthly burn is ~ $200K — please confirm which is correct.
- Goal: Improve margins and ensure scalable, capital-efficient growth. Immediate priorities: preserve optionality (extend runway), raise margins (gross + contribution), and scale revenue channels that keep CAC payback short.

Immediate (next 30 days) — preserve cash and buy time
- Reconcile runway/burn immediately (finance team): produce a 30/60/90-day cash forecast to confirm cash receipts, payables, and one-time commitments.
- Freeze non-essential hires and discretionary spend. Target 10–25% reduction in monthly op-ex to extend runway.
- Accelerate collections: tighten payment terms, push AR follow-up, offer small discounts for upfront annual payments.
- Pause or reprioritize low-ROI marketing programs; reallocate spend to highest-converting channels.
- Negotiate vendor contracts (payment terms, pricing). Ask for 60–90 day payment terms where possible.
- Hold noncritical capex and large one-time spends.

Short-to-medium term (30–120 days) — margin & revenue levers
- Pricing & packaging:
  - Run quick A/B pricing experiments on upsell/renewal offers.
  - Offer annual prepaid discounts (improves cash and reduces churn).
  - Repackage features into higher-margin bundles; push higher ARPA customers to enterprise plans.
- Improve gross margin:
  - Reduce COGS: renegotiate supplier rates, move to volume discounts, bring high-cost services in-house only when cheaper long-term.
  - Automate manual fulfillment/onboarding steps to lower cost-per-customer.
- Increase revenue efficiency:
  - Focus on expansion revenue: upsells, cross-sells to existing customers (lower CAC).
  - Tighten sales qualification to shorten sales cycles and increase conversion rate.
  - Improve onboarding to reduce churn and raise LTV.
- Reassess go-to-market channels: quantify CAC by channel and reallocate to channels with LTV:CAC > 3x and payback < 12 months.

Medium term (3–12 months) — scale with unit economics
- Invest in product and automation that reduce marginal cost per customer (self-serve, API-first).
- Build repeatable sales motions for enterprise accounts (higher ARPA) and a self-serve funnel for SMB (lower CAC).
- Track and optimize unit economics: contribution margin per customer, gross margin %, CAC, LTV, payback period.
- Consider strategic partnerships/resellers to scale distribution without proportionate increase in fixed costs.

Financial scenarios to test (recommended 12-month model)
- Baseline: current revenue growth and current burn. Verify runway.
- Cost-reduction: 15–25% operational expense reduction — show runway extension and impact on growth.
- Growth-investment: invest X% more in high-ROI channels (higher burn short-term) — show target take-rate, CAC payback and whether fundraising is needed.

Key metrics to include in the narrative & slides
- Cash on hand, runway (months) under each scenario
- Monthly burn (net cash burn)
- Revenue (TTM and LTM monthly cadence), MRR/ARR if applicable
- Gross margin % and contribution margin per customer
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio, CAC payback months
- Churn (NRR, logo churn, revenue churn)
- ARPA / ARPU, sales cycle length, average contract value (ACV)
- KPIs for next 90/180 days with owners

Suggested finance narrative (one-paragraph + bullets — ready for a slide)
Paragraph:
"Horizon Media begins this period with $1.2M of cash and strong top-line performance last quarter ($1.8M). Our immediate objective is to secure runway while improving margins and scaling revenue channels that deliver durable unit economics. Over the next 90 days we will tighten cash management, accelerate collections, optimize pricing and packaging, and redeploy marketing into the highest-ROI channels. These actions should extend our runway materially and improve contribution margins so we can either scale organically or go to market from a stronger negotiating position."

Bullets (next 90 days — owner / target)
- Confirm cash runway and reconcile burn (Finance / Day 3) — target: accurate 90-day cash forecast.
- Reduce discretionary OPEX by 15% (CFO / 30 days).
- Collect 20% of outstanding AR by offering 2–4% discount for 30- or 60-day prepayment (Sales/AR / 30 days).
- Run 2 pricing experiments to lift ACV by 10–20% (Product/Sales / 60 days).
- Reallocate marketing budget to top-2 channels by CAC efficiency (CMO / 45 days).
- Deliver 12-month scenario model for Board review (Finance / 30 days).

Quick example scenarios (illustrative — adjust once you confirm numbers)
- If burn really is $62K/month: runway ≈ 19 months. Focus on margin expansion and measured growth (lower urgency).
- If runway is 6 months (implied burn ≈ $200K/month): immediate cash preservation is critical — target cut to $150K/month ASAP via cost reductions and upfront collections, which would extend runway from 6 to 8 months while buying time to test revenue acceleration.

Deliverables I can prepare for you (pick one or more)
1. A 1-page finance narrative + executive summary slide (ready for execs/Board).
2. A 6–12 month cash-flow & scenario model showing runway under baseline / cost-cut / growth-invest scenarios.
3. A 6-slide investor/internal deck focused on margin improvement and scalability.
4. A draft email to send to the leadership team or Board summarizing the plan.

Next step
Tell me which deliverable(s) you want first and confirm the three clarifying items I asked (runway vs burn, revenue cash/recurring status, any quick breakdown of costs). If you want, I’ll start by drafting the 1-page finance narrative and a 3-scenario 12-month cash model — I just need confirmation of the numbers or permission to use the assumptions above.

