1) Quick data check (please confirm)
- You said: cash = $1.2M, runway = 6 months, burn = $62K/month, revenue last quarter = $1.8M.
- These don’t line up:
  - If burn = $62K/month, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months (not 6).
  - If runway = 6 months on $1.2M cash, implied burn = 1.2M / 6 = $200K/month (not $62K).
  - If burn = $62K/month and runway = 6 months, implied cash ≈ 62K * 6 = $372K (not $1.2M).
Please confirm which numbers are correct (cash vs burn vs runway) and whether “burn” is net burn (cash out minus operating cash in) or gross cash burn (total outflows).

2) Useful derived numbers (based on what you gave)
- Annualized revenue (run rate) = $1.8M * 4 = $7.2M.
- Current runway (if cash = $1.2M and burn = $62K) ≈ 19 months — gives much more optionality than a 6-month runway.

3) Key metrics & KPIs to include in the finance narrative
- Cash and runway (explicitly state how burn is calculated)
- Monthly/Quarterly revenue, ARR/MRR (if applicable), and YoY/QoQ growth rates
- Gross margin and COGS breakdown
- Contribution margin per product/customer
- EBITDA / operating margin
- CAC (customer acquisition cost), LTV (customer lifetime value), LTV:CAC ratio
- Churn (revenue & customer) and retention cohorts
- Burn multiple (net burn / net new ARR) if you’re growing
- Customer concentration and top-10 customers’ % of revenue
- Working capital (AR days, AP days, inventory if any)

4) High-impact margin-improvement levers (prioritized)
Immediate / low friction (quick wins)
- Reduce discretionary spend (freeze non-critical hires, travel, consulting) — target 5–15% OPEX cut quickly.
- Accelerate collections (shorter payment terms, more rigorous AR follow-up, early-pay discounts) — immediate cash effect.
- Extend payables where possible (negotiate supplier terms +15–30 days).
- Re-negotiate vendor contracts (cloud, ad tech, software, media buys) and consolidate vendors.

Medium-term / structural
- Improve pricing and packaging: test price increases, introduce premium tiers, remove deep discounting for new business.
- Shift sales incentives to promote higher-margin products or bundles.
- Reduce content/production costs by optimizing workflows, in-sourcing vs out-sourcing analysis.
- Improve gross margin by changing supplier mix or renegotiating rates with content providers/platforms.

Longer-term / strategic
- Productize services to reduce labor intensity (create repeatable offerings, automation, templates).
- Invest in tech and automation (ad ops, billing, reporting) to scale revenue without linear headcount increases.
- Focus on customer retention & expansion (land-and-expand, upsells) — lower CAC payback and improve LTV.

5) Scalability actions (to grow revenue while controlling costs)
- Modularize operations: standardized playbooks, templates, and SOPs for onboarding and campaign management.
- Build shared services (centralized finance, ops, analytics) so incremental revenue adds less incremental cost.
- Invest in instrumentation / analytics to measure CAC by channel and true profitability by client.
- Hire selectively: prioritize revenue-generating and automation roles; delay generalist hires.
- Platform investments: build or buy tools that automate repetitive tasks (reporting, ad optimization).
- Channel diversification: reduce customer concentration and smooth revenue.

6) Concrete example scenarios (illustrative)
- If you can reduce OPEX by 15% (non-headcount discretionary) and improve gross margin by 3–5 percentage points, EBITDA can move materially toward breakeven within 6–12 months.
- Example impact (if you confirm current P&L numbers, I can do real modeling):
  - Reduce monthly burn from $62K to $50K (20% cut) → with $1.2M cash runway extends from ~19 months to ~24 months.
  - If actual burn is $200K/mo (per 6-month runway), a 20% reduction to $160K/month extends runway from 6 to 7.5 months and lowers near-term liquidity risk.

7) Finance narrative — short draft you can use / adapt
"Horizon Media finished the last quarter with $1.2M in cash and a quarterly revenue run-rate of $7.2M. We are focused on improving margins and scaling revenue efficiently by prioritizing high-margin products, tightening discretionary spend, and investing selectively in automation to remove manual operational costs. Near term, we are implementing receivables acceleration and vendor-term renegotiations to preserve liquidity. Over 12–18 months, our plan targets a X percentage-point gross margin improvement and an EBITDA path to breakeven through a combination of pricing optimization, productization of services, and top-line expansion into higher-margin segments."

