- You said: cash = $1.2M, runway = 6 months, burn rate = $62K/month, revenue last quarter = $1.8M.
- Those three numbers are inconsistent as given:
  - Monthly revenue (last quarter) = $1.8M / 3 = $600K/month (annualizes to $7.2M).
  - If burn = $62K/month, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months (not 6 months).
  - If runway = 6 months, implied burn = 1.2M / 6 = $200K/month (not $62K).

Please confirm:
1) Is the $62K/month burn your net cash burn after revenue (i.e., negative operating cash flow), or is it only operating expenses excluding revenue?  
2) Is the 6-month runway a target/requirement (e.g., you want to preserve 6 months) or an observed metric today?  

Once you confirm, I’ll tailor the narrative precisely. Meanwhile, below are immediate, actionable recommendations and a ready-to-use finance narrative you can adapt.

Quick financial snapshot (using the inputs as stated)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter): $600K
- Cash: $1.2M
- If burn = $62K/mo → runway ≈ 19.4 months (comfortable)
- If runway = 6 months → implied burn ≈ $200K/mo (urgent cash conservation)

High-level approach (priorities)
1. Fix any data/definition inconsistencies (as above). Agree on what “burn” means for your team.  
2. Short-term (0–90 days) — preserve cash & shore up margins:
   - Quick pricing review: test a +5–10% price increase for non-contractual/renewal customers or introduce small packaging increases. Small changes on $600K/month revenue: +5% = +$30K/month incremental gross.  
   - Cut discretionary spend: freeze nonessential hiring, hiring backfills only for revenue-critical roles, pause noncritical projects.  
   - Vendor/COGS renegotiation: push for better terms, volume discounts, or delayed payments. A 2–5% COGS reduction on $600K = $12–30K/month.  
   - Improve collections & AR: tighten payment terms, incentives for early pay, reduce DSO to free working capital.  
   - Re-price high-cost accounts or move to fixed-contracts where possible.  
3. Medium-term (3–12 months) — structural margin improvement and scalable growth:
   - Productize services to move from custom/low-margin work to repeatable, higher-margin offerings.  
   - Increase gross margin by shifting mix toward higher-margin products/services (focus on upsell, bundles, add-ons).  
   - Invest in automation (billing, onboarding, customer success) to reduce marginal cost per customer as you scale.  
   - Rebuild go-to-market (GTM) efficiency: reduce CAC, focus on highest-LTV segments, formalize sales playbooks.  
4. Finance & capital levers:
   - Build scenario cash models (Base, Upside, Downside) and set triggers for each (hiring freeze, fundraising).  
   - Consider short-term financing options if runway is below target: venture bridge, RBF, credit line, or delayed vendor payments.  
   - Monitor KPIs weekly: Cash balance, runway (real-time), gross margin%, EBITDA, MRR/ARR (if applicable), CAC, LTV, churn, DSO.

Estimated impact examples (illustrative)
- +5% pricing on $600K = +$30K/month gross (covers ~48% of a $62K burn).  
- 3% COGS reduction on $600K = +$18K/month.  
- Combined small actions (5% price + 3% COGS + 10% reduction in discretionary expenses) could swing cash by $60–100K/month.

Key KPIs to include in the finance narrative
- Cash balance and runway (explicit assumptions)
- Monthly Recurring Revenue / Monthly Revenue (current vs. trend)
- Gross margin % (current and target)
- Net burn / EBITDA / operating cash flow
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio
- Churn (revenue and customer)
- DSO / Days payable

Suggested one-page finance narrative (editable)
- Current position: “Horizon Media ended Q[ ] with $1.2M cash and generated $1.8M in revenue last quarter (≈$600K/month). We currently have X months of runway under our defined burn assumptions. Our immediate priority is to extend runway while intentionally investing in scalable revenue channels that increase gross margins.”
- Short-term goals (0–90 days): “(1) Secure cash runway to at least 9–12 months through pricing and cost actions; (2) implement quick margin levers: a targeted +5% price test, renegotiate COGS and vendor terms, accelerate AR; (3) freeze non-critical hires and reduce discretionary spend.”
- Medium-term goals (3–12 months): “(1) Productize higher-margin services and migrate at least 30% of revenue to these offerings; (2) reduce COGS by X percentage points via supplier negotiation and efficiency; (3) optimize GTM to improve CAC payback time to <12 months and raise LTV:CAC to >3x.”
- Asks of leadership: “Approve a 90-day cash preservation plan (pricing tests, hiring freeze exceptions), allocate a small capital budget for automation that reduces marginal service cost, and schedule a weekly cash & KPIs review.”
- Metrics & next steps: “We will produce weekly cash & KPI updates, and a 3-scenario (base/upside/downside) 12-month cash model by [date].”

90-day action plan (concise)
- Week 1: Align definitions (burn/runway), finalize KPI dashboard.  
- Weeks 1–3: Launch pricing test on a subset, start vendor renegotiations, freeze discretionary hiring.  
- Weeks 3–6: Implement AR terms changes, roll out initial automation pilot to reduce manual costs.  
- Weeks 6–12: Evaluate pricing test; if successful, roll out; build productized offerings roadmap; finalize scenario models.

Would you like me to:
- (A) Draft a one-page internal memo using the confirmed numbers?  
- (B) Create a 3-scenario 12-month cash model and slide (Base / Upside / Downside)?  
- (C) Draft an email to the leadership team proposing the 90-day plan?

Tell me which deliverable you want and confirm the burn/runway definition (and whether $1.8M is the most recent quarter). I can draft the memo or model next.

