Thanks — I can help. Before I build slides or a model, one quick clarification: the numbers you gave are inconsistent. With $1.2M cash:

- If burn = $62K/month, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19 months.
- If runway = 6 months, implied burn ≈ $200K/month.

Which is correct: the $62K burn figure, or the 6-month runway? I’ll proceed with both interpretations below and give clear, actionable levers and a finance narrative you can use. Tell me which runway/burn number is accurate and whether you want a short slide-style executive summary, a one-page memo, or a 12-month cash-flow model next.

Immediate summary (pick the right baseline after you confirm the burn/runway):
- If burn = $62K/month: cash position is healthy (≈19 months runway). Focus: margin expansion and scalable growth investments.
- If runway = 6 months (implied burn ≈ $200K/month): urgent action required — prioritize cash preservation, quick margin wins, and near-term revenue acceleration or bridge funding.

Recommended structure for the finance narrative (short version you can use in internal materials)
1) Current state: concise facts (cash, runway, monthly burn, last quarter revenue). Example:
   “Horizon Media enters the quarter with $1.2M cash and X months of runway, having generated $1.8M in revenue last quarter. Our current monthly cash burn is $YK.”
2) Key issue/priority:
   - If runway short: “Primary focus is to extend runway to 12 months through a mix of margin improvements, cost optimization, and near-term revenue acceleration.”
   - If runway long: “Primary focus is to increase margins and invest in scalable, recurring revenue channels while preserving >12 months’ runway.”
3) Plan (3–6 months): list top 4–6 initiatives (see recommended actions below).
4) Targets & KPIs: specific numeric targets for margin, revenue growth, burn reduction, and runway extension.
5) Ask: approvals, headcount/funding reallocation, or board/investor engagement needed.
6) Next deliverable: 12-month cash flow scenarios (base, downside, upside) and an action tracker.

Concrete levers to improve margin and scalability (with expected impact ranges)
1) Pricing & packaging (impact: ↑gross margin 3–10 pp)
   - Move clients to value-based or performance pricing when possible (premium for guaranteed outcomes).
   - Introduce retainer/contracted services vs one-off buys to stabilize revenue and improve LTV.
   - Bundle services to increase ARPU and reduce sales cycles.

2) Productize & standardize delivery (impact: Opex ↓10–30% per engagement)
   - Create standard packages and templates to reduce bespoke work.
   - Use playbooks for common campaign types to lower delivery time and headcount needs.
   - Convert services into repeatable, higher-margin offerings (analytics subscription, campaign ops).

3) Media buying & supplier renegotiation (impact: ↑margin 2–8 pp)
   - Negotiate better rates with top vendors, or shift to programmatic buying where margins are clearer.
   - Consolidate vendors to increase negotiating leverage.
   - Introduce performance-sharing agreements with publishers/platforms.

4) Cost base optimization (impact: cash burn ↓10–40%)
   - Immediate: freeze non-essential hiring, cut discretionary spend (events, travel).
   - Near-term: audit SaaS/contract spend and eliminate underused subscriptions.
   - Medium-term: consider limited restructuring or reallocation of roles into higher-value functions.

5) Improve client mix and retention (impact: revenue ↑5–20%, CAC/LTV improvement)
   - Focus sales on higher-margin verticals and client segments.
   - Create upsell/cross-sell motions (data services, measurement).
   - Implement account-based retention programs and early churn warnings.

6) Operational automation (impact: Opex ↓5–15%; scalable capacity)
   - Invest selectively in automation for campaign setup/reporting.
   - Outsource lower-margin, repetitive tasks to lower-cost providers or partners.

7) Cash & working capital actions (impact: immediate runway extension)
   - Tighten billing and collections (reduce DSO).
   - Negotiate extended payment terms with suppliers.
   - Consider short-term bridge financing or a convertible note if runway truly <9 months.

Suggested KPIs to report weekly/monthly
- Cash on hand and runway (months)
- Monthly cash burn (and trend)
- Revenue (monthly & LTM) and YoY/QoQ change
- Gross margin and contribution margin
- EBITDA margin
- Revenue by client and % of total (concentration risk)
- Recurring revenue % (MRR or retainer revenue)
- CAC, LTV, churn (if applicable)
- Revenue per FTE

Three actionable 90-day plan (high level)
Week 0–2: Reconcile numbers & stabilize
- Confirm true burn/runway and build a high-level 6–12 month cash forecast.
- Immediate cost triage: freeze discretionary spend and hiring.
Week 3–8: Quick margin wins
- Reprice top 20% clients where pricing lags.
- Renegotiate top vendor contracts and reduce SaaS waste.
- Launch 2 productized service offerings to increase ARPU.
Week 9–12: Scale and measure
- Deploy automation in delivery (one or two highest-impact processes).
- Implement retention/upsell program for top accounts.
- Review results and set next quarter operating plan; if runway still short, open funding conversations.

Example numeric scenario (to illustrate what’s needed to reach 12-month runway)
- Baseline ambiguity: if burn = $200K/month (6-month runway):
  Option A (cost focus): Reduce burn 40% → new burn = $120K → runway = 1.2M / 120K = 10 months (close to goal).
  Option B (revenue focus): Increase monthly revenue by 30% (if margins hold) → added operating cashflow that offsets burn and extends runway.
  A combined approach of 20% cost reduction + 15% revenue increase will likely achieve 12-month runway.
- If burn = $62K/month: focus on margin improvement opportunities above; runway already ≈19 months but still improve margins for future scaling.

Short sample executive summary paragraph you can paste into a memo
“Horizon Media has $1.2M in cash. Over the next quarter our priority is to improve margins and scale sustainably. We will reconcile monthly burn and build 3 cash scenarios, implement immediate cost controls, reprice and productize higher-margin services, and accelerate retention/upsell motions for existing clients. Our target in 90 days is to improve contribution margins by 5–8 percentage points and either extend runway to ≥12 months or secure a short-term funding bridge if needed.”

Next steps I can take for you (tell me which you want)
- Draft a 1-page internal memo or slide deck summarizing the plan (I can draft language and slide bullets).
- Build a 12-month cash-flow model with base/downside/upside scenarios (I’ll need monthly revenue, gross margin, fixed/variable cost breakdown, AR/AP, and any planned hires or one-time costs).
- Draft the email to the CEO/CFO/board with the finance narrative and ask (I’ll include data, actions, and the ask).
- Run a deeper client / revenue analysis (need revenue by client, margin by client).

Which deliverable do you want first, and please confirm which burn/runway number is accurate and any missing data you can share (monthly revenue, gross margin %, fixed costs, headcount expenses, major contracts or receivables >30 days)?

