Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. First, one quick clarification so my recommendations and the narrative are accurate:

- You said Horizon has $1.2M cash, burn $62K/month, and 6 months runway. Those three numbers aren’t consistent:
  - If burn = $62K/month, runway = $1.2M / $62K ≈ 19.4 months.
  - If runway = 6 months, implied burn = $1.2M / 6 = $200K/month.
  - Revenue last quarter $1.8M → monthly revenue ≈ $600K/month.

Which of these is the correct definition of “burn” for your note? (Is $62K the net burn after revenue? operating cash outflow? Or is $62K something else—e.g., payroll-only burn?) Tell me which is correct and whether the $1.8M is recognized revenue or cash collected last quarter (and whether seasonality or receivables are an issue). Meanwhile I’ll proceed with two quick scenarios + concrete margin & scalability recommendations and a short finance narrative you can use.

Quick math (two interpretations)
- If $62K net cash burn/month:
  - Runway = $1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (good cushion).
  - Monthly revenue (last quarter) ≈ $600K → you’re likely cash-positive on operations or close to break-even after revenue unless there’s a timing/receivable issue.
- If runway = 6 months (implied burn = $200K/month):
  - You need to cut burn to $100K/month or raise $1.2M to get to a 12-month runway.
  - With monthly revenue ≈ $600K, you may have issues with margins, collections, or one-off large outflows.

Immediate margin-improvement levers (0–90 days)
- Cash & collections
  - Accelerate collections: tighten payment terms, offer small discounts for early pay, prioritize AR follow-up.
  - Stretch payables moderately—negotiate payment terms with key vendors.
- Quick cost cuts (target 10–30% of controllable spend)
  - Pause non-critical hiring; defer large non-urgent projects.
  - Cut low-ROI marketing channels; reallocate to highest-LTV/lowest-CAC channels.
  - Audit SaaS & vendor spend; cancel redundant subscriptions and renegotiate contracts.
  - Implement hiring freeze + review contractors; convert fixed costs to variable where possible.
- Revenue/margin uplift
  - Focus on high-margin products/services and upsell to existing customers.
  - Short-term price increases for low-elasticity segments or value-add bundles.
  - Improve discounting governance and contract terms to protect margins.
- Operational
  - Improve gross margin: renegotiate supplier prices, source lower-cost inputs, reduce returns/waste.
  - Reduce manual processes with targeted automation to cut operating expense per transaction.

Medium-term scalability (3–12 months)
- Product & GTM
  - Move toward recurring revenue (subscription/retainer) and multi-year contracts where possible.
  - Build repeatable sales playbooks and self-serve onboarding to reduce CAC and cost-to-serve.
  - Invest selectively in automation to scale support and delivery without linear headcount growth.
- Unit economics & metrics
  - Optimize CAC payback — prioritize channels with faster payback and higher LTV.
  - Segment customers by margin and double-down on high-margin cohorts.
- Strategic partnerships
  - Use channel partners and platform integrations to expand reach without expanding fixed cost base.
- Cost architecture
  - Shift fixed costs to variable (outsourcing, usage-based cloud) to preserve runway as you scale.

Key KPIs to include in the finance narrative
- Cash runway (months) — explicitly show assumptions
- Net burn (cash outflow minus cash inflow) — monthly
- Revenue run-rate (MRR/ARR equivalent) and QoQ growth %
- Gross margin and contribution margin
- CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio, CAC payback period
- Churn (revenue and customer), upsell rate, revenue per customer
- Revenue per employee, operating expense breakdown (fixed vs variable)
- Days sales outstanding (DSO) and days payables outstanding (DPO)

Scenario examples (illustrative)
- Current (if burn = $200K/month, 6-month runway):
  - To reach 12 months without raising capital, cut burn to $100K/month (50% reduction) or raise ~$1.2M.
- Targeted cost reductions:
  - 20% cut of controllable operating costs → if controllable portion = $120K of the $200K burn, 20% saves $24K → runway extends by ~0.5 months on a $1.2M base. Combine multiple levers for meaningful extension.
- Revenue-led extension:
  - Increase monthly revenue by 25% (from $600K to $750K) through upsell/pricing → contribution margin improvement may let you convert incremental revenue to cash quickly, reducing net burn.

Suggested 1-page finance narrative (short)
- Executive summary (1–2 lines): “Horizon Media enters Q2 with $1.2M cash. Under current assumptions we have X months of runway; priority is to extend runway to 12 months while investing in high-return growth initiatives.”
- Current position (facts + one-sentence interpretation): cash, burn, last quarter revenue, receivables situation.
- Immediate actions (next 30–90 days): tighten AR, pause nonessential hires, 10–20% cut in low-ROI spend, renegotiate vendor terms, prioritize high-margin sales.
- Medium-term plan (3–12 months): shift to recurring revenue, invest in automation & sales efficiency, pursue strategic partnerships.
- KPIs & reporting: list the 6–8 KPIs above and cadence (weekly cash, monthly KPIs).
- Ask or decision required from leadership: e.g., “Approval to implement up to 20% discretionary spend reduction and hiring freeze; authority to renegotiate or cancel non-critical vendor commitments.”

Suggested talking points for leadership
- “We have a clear plan to extend runway to 12 months through a mix of immediate cost discipline and prioritized growth investments. We will track cash weekly and report three scenarios (base, downside, upside) each month.”
- “We will protect revenue-generating activities and high-margin products while pausing non-essential projects.”

Next steps I can do for you
- Reconcile the numbers and produce a 1-page finance memo and a 3-slide deck for leadership (current state, 90-day plan, scenarios). Tell me whether burn = $62K or $200K and whether the $1.8M was cash collected.
- Build a simple scenario model (base / downside / upside) with explicit assumptions so you can show runway under each.
- Draft an internal email to staff or the board summarizing the plan.

Which deliverable do you want next (memo, slide deck, board email, or scenario model)? And please confirm the correct definition of burn/runway and whether $1.8M is cash collected.

