Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. First, a quick check on the numbers, then practical margin-improvement and scalability levers, a prioritized action plan, example financial scenarios, suggested KPIs to track, and short sample finance narratives you can use for the board or internal teams.

1) Immediate check: numbers you gave
- Cash = $1.2M
- Burn = $62K/month
- Runway claimed = 6 months
- Revenue last quarter = $1.8M (=> monthly revenue ≈ $600K)

Quick math:
- If burn = $62K/mo, runway = $1,200,000 / $62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (not 6).
- If runway is actually 6 months, implied burn = $1,200,000 / 6 = $200K/month (not $62K).
Please confirm which of the two is correct (burn = $62K or runway = 6 months). I’ll assume the numbers might be inconsistent and will give guidance for both situations where useful.

2) Topline context and implication
- With quarterly revenue $1.8M (≈ $600K/month), a $62K burn is very healthy (net burn ≈ 10% of monthly revenue). With that burn you have long runway.
- If actual burn is ~$200K/mo then cash runway is short and requires urgent action (200K is ~33% of monthly revenue), so focus should be on immediate cash preservation + quick margin wins.

3) Margin-improvement levers (practical, prioritized)
A. Revenue-side (higher-margin revenue)
  - Price optimization: test modest price increases for new customers and high-touch upsell for existing customers; target a 3–7% price lift where possible.
  - Product mix: push higher-margin products/services (SKU rationalization, packaging to encourage higher ARPU).
  - Upsell / cross-sell & retention: increase attach rates, add premium tiers, shorten sales cycles for existing clients.
  - Reduce discounts and tighten contract terms (minimum contract lengths, prepayment discounts).

B. Cost-of-goods / delivery
  - Negotiate vendor and supplier contracts (target 5–15% savings on large vendors).
  - Move low-value work to lower-cost providers or automate repetitive tasks (RPA, templates).
  - Reassess sub-contractors / consultants and vendor SLAs for efficiency.

C. Operating expense (fast wins)
  - Hiring freeze for non-critical roles; repurpose hires to revenue-generating tasks.
  - Cut or renegotiate discretionary spending (travel, events, marketing trial spend).
  - Freeze or renegotiate office/site costs where possible (flex leases, remote).

D. Structural / strategic (scale)
  - Productize bespoke services to reduce delivery time and increase margins.
  - Move to recurring revenue models (subscriptions, retainers).
  - Standardize onboarding and implementation to lower customer acquisition and delivery costs (reduce COGS per customer).
  - Invest selectively in scalable sales channels (partnerships, platform integrations).

4) Example dollar impact scenarios (useful for your narrative)
Assumptions: quarterly revenue = $1.8M (monthly = $600K).
Scenario A — modest margin improvements (short-term):
  - Improve gross margin by +5 percentage points on revenue.
    - Quarterly uplift = 0.05 * $1.8M = $90K => ~ $30K/month improvement to gross profit.
  - Reduce operating spend by $40K/month (hiring freeze + discretionary cuts).
    - Combined monthly improvement = $70K.
If your burn was $200K/mo, this brings burn down to ~$130K/mo and extends runway from 6 months to ~9.2 months (1.2M / 130K).

Scenario B — more aggressive (90-day target)
  - Gross margin +10 pts = +$180K/quarter (~$60K/mo).
  - Opex cuts = $60K/mo.
  - Combined = $120K/month improvement.
If burn was $200K, that could get you near break-even (200 - 120 = 80K burn), runway extends to ~15 months.

If your burn is actually $62K:
  - Even modest margin improvements give excess cushion for growth investments; focus more on scaling efficiently (CAC payback, expanding ARR) than urgent cuts.

5) KPIs to include in the finance narrative (must-have)
- Cash balance and runway (months) under baseline and stress scenarios
- Net burn (cash outflow) per month
- Revenue run-rate (MRR/ARR if applicable) and revenue growth % QoQ
- Gross margin % and gross profit dollars
- Contribution margin per product/customer
- CAC, LTV, payback months
- Customer concentration (top 10 customers %)
- AR days / collection trends and deferred revenue
- Operating expense breakdown (people vs. non-people)
- Forecast vs actual (variance analysis)

6) 90-day prioritized plan (what to do now)
Immediate (days 0–14)
  - Reconcile cash/runway numbers (confirm burn formula and outstanding commitments).
  - Produce a 13-week cash flow forecast and a downside scenario (30% revenue drop, 20% slower collections).
  - Put a temporary hiring freeze and review all open requisitions.

Short term (30–90 days)
  - Negotiate large vendor contracts and key leases.
  - Execute quick revenue levers: pricing tests, targeted upsells to top customers.
  - Drive AR collections: ask for prepayments, shorten payment terms.
  - Cut discretionary spend and pause low-ROI marketing.

Medium term (90–180 days)
  - Productize services to improve margins.
  - Invest in automation for delivery and support.
  - Re-evaluate go-to-market to scale high-margin segments.

7) Sample finance narratives
A. Short investor-style (one-liner)
"Horizon Media holds $1.2M in cash with $1.8M last quarter revenue. We are executing a two-phase profitability plan: immediate cost optimization and AR acceleration to extend runway, followed by productization and pricing optimization to lift gross margins and sustainably scale."

B. Board update (2–3 paragraphs)
"Horizon generated $1.8M in revenue last quarter and currently holds $1.2M in cash. We are running a focused program to strengthen margins and extend runway. Step one (30–90 days) will target a 5–10% uplift in gross margin via pricing and product mix plus $40–60K/mo in operating cost reductions through hiring controls and vendor renegotiations. This conserves cash and buys time to execute higher-impact structural changes.
Step two (90–180 days) will standardize delivery, convert bespoke work into repeatable, higher-margin offerings, and expand recurring revenue channels. We will report weekly on cash, net burn, gross margin, and AR days. Our target: move to positive cash flow or reduce net burn by at least 60% within 90 days and reach a sustainable gross margin profile that supports scalable growth."

C. Internal ops/tactical (bullet points)
- Target: +5% gross margin in 60 days, -20% discretionary OpEx in 90 days.
- Actions: pricing tests, contract renegotiations, hiring freeze, AR acceleration, product packaging.
- Metrics: runway (weeks), net burn, gross margin %, CAC payback.

8) Ask / next steps I can do for you
- I can build a one-page financial model showing baseline and 2 stress scenarios (fast/slow) and produce a slide-ready one-page board summary.
- To do that I’ll need: current P&L (last 12 months), monthly cashflow and cash balance, AR/AP aging, top 10 customers revenue, headcount costs, major contract commitments, and growth assumptions.

Which would you like me to do next?
- Reconcile your runway (confirm actual burn vs claimed 6 months) and build the 13-week cash forecast?
- Build the 3-scenario model and one-page board slide?
- Draft a 1-page finance narrative/email for your next board meeting?

Tell me which and share the data you prefer me to use (or confirm your burn number), and I’ll prepare the model/narrative.

