  - If burn = $62K/month, runway = 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months (not 6 months).
  - If runway = 6 months, implied burn = 1.2M / 6 = $200K/month (not $62K).
  - Monthly revenue (last quarter) = 1.8M / 3 = $600K/month.
Please confirm whether the $62K figure is (a) net cash burn after revenues, (b) payroll-only or something else, and whether the 6‑month runway is calculated after reserved capital or planned spend.

2) Immediate things to do (next 7–30 days)
- Reconcile cash flow and runway calculation now (confirm the correct burn). I can make a quick 3-scenario model once you confirm figures.
- Pause or freeze all non-critical hiring and discretionary spend immediately.
- Negotiate near-term vendor deferrals (payments, SaaS credits, lower tiers).
- Convert customers to upfront or annual billing where possible (improves cash).
- Prioritize collections: push on AR, shorten DSO, require deposits for high-risk customers.
- Quick margin wins: cut marketing spend to top-performing channels, shift to performance-based spend, trim inefficient customer acquisition channels.

3) Medium-term margin & scalability levers (1–6 months)
- Cost structure
  - Move fixed cost to variable: outsource or convert FTEs to contractors for non-core work.
  - Consolidate/renegotiate office leases, software subscriptions, and vendor contracts.
  - Trim G&A and discretionary travel/consulting by 15–30% target.
- Revenue and price
  - Price optimization: test a 5–10% raise on under-priced segments; introduce packaging that increases ARPU (tiered pricing, add-ons).
  - Upsell and cross-sell: create targeted plays for top 20% customers (often 60–80% of revenue).
  - Enterprise deals with upfront payments or longer terms; sell multi-quarter deals with discounts for prepayment.
- Unit economics & GTM
  - Measure CAC and LTV by cohort. If CAC payback > 12 months, tighten acquisition or improve onboarding to reduce churn.
  - Invest in self-service funnels and automation to lower cost-to-serve as volume scales.
  - Channel/partner expansion to reduce direct sales costs and accelerate reach.
- Product & ops
  - Productize services into higher-margin, repeatable offerings.
  - Automate manual finance and customer success tasks to lower operational cost per customer.
  - Standardize onboarding to reduce time-to-revenue.

4) Targeted KPI changes to show in your finance narrative
- Revenue growth rate (QoQ and YoY)
- Gross margin and contribution margin (per product/customer)
- Net burn (cash outflow per month) and runway months
- CAC, LTV, CAC payback period
- AR days / DSO and churn rate
- Revenue per customer (ARPU) and top-customer concentration
- Operating leverage: % of revenue absorbed by fixed costs

5) Suggested finance narrative (short, board/executive-ready)
- Opening summary: “Horizon Media has $1.2M cash. Last quarter revenue $1.8M (≈ $600K/mo). Based on current assumptions [confirm burn], runway is X months. We must improve margins and scale revenue to reach sustainable profitability or extend runway prior to next financing.”
- Diagnosis: “Primary margin pressure comes from [list largest expense categories]. Sales efficiency shows CAC payback of Y months and churn of Z%.”
- Plan (90-day priorities): “1) Immediate cost control (hiring freeze, vendor re-negotiations). 2) Cash acceleration (AR, prepayments). 3) Revenue acceleration (pricing, enterprise upsells, channel partnerships). 4) Build repeatable operational model for scale (automation, productization).”
- Targets: “Reduce net burn by 30% in 90 days; increase ARPU by 10% in next quarter; extend runway to 12 months (or lower fundraising need to $X).”
- Ask: “Approve temporary hiring freeze and a $X contingency; authorize sales to push annual/prepaid contracts; prepare a fundraising/bridge plan if runway < 9 months.”

