- Regardless of the scenario, tightening metrics and narrative is critical: investors and the board want crisp KPIs, three scenarios (base/conservative/optimistic), and a prioritized plan with owners and timelines.

Prioritized plan to improve margins and enable scalable growth

A. Quick wins (2–6 weeks) — low effort, immediate cash or margin impact
- Vendor & subscription audit: cancel unused SaaS, consolidate vendors, renegotiate top 10 vendor contracts. Potential savings: $10–50K/month depending on scale.
- Hiring freeze + contract/comp consultant review: pause nonessential hires; convert some FTE plans to contractors. Saves immediate monthly payroll spend.
- Discounting & collections: tighten payment terms, accelerate AR collections (early-pay discounts), and reduce DSO. Improves cash flow quickly.
- Marketing spend reallocation: pause underperforming channels; shift to higher-ROI channels and demand gen for existing accounts.
- Quick price/packaging tweaks for high-margin upsells: small price increases or remove loss-leading offers.

B. Medium-term actions (1–3 months) — moderate effort, solid impact
- COGS/Gross margin optimization: renegotiate supplier pricing, bundle services, move to higher-margin suppliers or reduce direct costs per unit.
- Product-led growth / Self-serve funnel: reduce sales cost per new customer (CAC) by improving onboarding and trial-to-paid conversion.
- Customer success expansion: systematic cross-sell and upsell campaigns to existing customers (higher LTV, lower CAC).
- Process automation: billing, financial close, and support automation to reduce FTE hours.
- Right-size office/real estate footprint or increase remote flexibility to reduce fixed overhead.

C. Longer-term / strategic (3–12 months) — higher effort, high upside
- Introduce tiered pricing and enterprise plans (higher ACV and margins).
- Expand channel/partnership sales to scale without equivalent headcount increase.
- Product rationalization: sunset low-margin or high-support products.
- Invest in platform/tech to reduce marginal cost of serving customers (scale revenue without linear cost growth).
- Strategic M&A or JV to acquire higher-margin capabilities or distribution.

