2) Key KPIs to include and track
- Monthly Revenue (MR) and QoQ growth
- Gross margin % (revenue minus COGS) and gross profit $  
- Operating expense run-rate (SG&A, R&D, G&A) and burn (net cash change)  
- Cash runway (cash / net burn) — specify net burn definition  
- Contribution margin per product/customer (rev - direct variable costs)  
- LTV, CAC, CAC payback, churn (if recurring), ARPU / ARPC  
- Burn multiple (net burn over revenue growth) and EBITDA margin

3) Quick diagnostic (what to ask internally)
- Is the $1.8M recurring or one‑time (seasonal/contract)?  
- Is the $62K number net of revenue or gross OPEX?  
- Any near-term large commitments (debt payments, CapEx, contract renewals)?  
- COGS and main variable costs breakdown (ad spend, hosting, fulfillment, royalties, etc.)  
- Top 20 customers and concentration risk

4) Margin improvement levers (organized, with estimated potential)
Revenue-side (higher-margin and higher-scalability)
- Pricing optimization: test +3–8% price increases, or introduce tiered pricing — potential +2–6% revenue uplift.  
- Product mix & upsell: prioritize higher-margin services, push packaged offers — +3–10% margin improvement depending on mix.  
- Expand recurring revenue: convert one-time customers to subscriptions or retainers — big multiplier on predictability.  
- Channel & partnership scaling: use partners/resellers to scale sales without proportional sales headcount increase.

Cost-side (direct impact to margins)
- Reduce COGS: renegotiate suppliers, consolidate vendors, optimize procurement — typical savings 3–10% of COGS.  
- Automation / process improvement: move repetitive work to tooling or outsourcing — reduces variable labor costs and reduces ramp time for sales.  
- Marketing & CAC optimization: shift spend to highest-converting channels; reduce ineffective channels — improves payback and LTV/CAC ratio.  
- Headcount & fixed-cost discipline: hiring freeze for non-critical roles, attrition swaps, role consolidation — short-term OPEX reduction.  
- Cloud/hosting cost optimization: use reserved instances, rightsizing, caching — 10–30% savings in many cases.

Operational levers for scalability
- Productize services: convert bespoke work into repeatable packages with standardized pricing and onboarding.  
- Standardized onboarding & success playbooks to lower time-to-value and reduce churn.  
- Sales enablement & inside sales: deploy inside sales / SDR layer to scale pipeline efficiently.  
- KPI dashboards and unit economics per product to guide investments.  
- Invest selectively in automation and platform features that have clear top-line or cost ROI.

Estimated impact examples (illustrative)
- Price +4% on $600K/month = +$24K/month revenue (gross).  
- Reduce vendor/COGS by 5% on relevant spend — could add several thousand to tens of thousands/month depending on COGS base.  
- Reduce discretionary OPEX by 10% (if OPEX = 200K/month) = $20K/month savings.  
Combine a few of these and you can materially improve margins within 1–3 quarters.

5) Short finance narrative (two-sentence executive summary + 3 bullets)
Executive summary:
"Horizon Media finished last quarter with $1.8M revenue and $1.2M cash on hand. To extend runway and improve margins, we will focus on higher‑margin product mix, targeted pricing changes, and OPEX simplification while scaling revenue via partner channels and productization."

Top-line bullets for a slide:
- Current position: $1.2M cash; last quarter revenue $1.8M (avg $600K/month). Need to reconcile reported 6‑month runway with operating metrics (clarify net burn).  
- Immediate priorities (0–90 days): (1) validate revenue recurrence & top customer risk, (2) run pricing tests on top 20 accounts, (3) identify top 3 vendor negotiations and freeze non-critical hires.  
- 90–180 day plan: productize two services, implement onboarding playbook to reduce churn and time-to-value, migrate discretionary systems to cost-efficient cloud settings.  
- Financial targets: improve gross margin by 3–8pp; reduce OPEX by 5–12% (depending on headcount/vendor levers); extend runway by X months once net burn is confirmed.

6) Risks & mitigations (short)
- Risk: revenue drop or contract non-renewals → mitigation: prioritize account retention, diversify top customer exposure.  
- Risk: cost reductions impair delivery → mitigation: prioritize automation and process improvements rather than one-time headcount cuts in core delivery.

