Length: 7038 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a good starting point. A few quick clarifying items, then a recommended plan (actions you can take now), and a short finance narrative / slide outline you can use in internal materials.

1) Clarification of the numbers
- Monthly revenue (from your last quarter): $1.8M / 3 = $600k/month.
- Cash: $1.2M.
- You said runway = 6 months. That implies net cash burn ≈ $1.2M / 6 = $200k/month.
- You also said burn = $62k/month. Those two figures conflict: if net burn is $62k/month, runway would be ~19 months (1.2M / 62k ≈ 19.4 months). Please confirm whether:
  - $62k is gross burn (operating cash outflow before revenue), or
  - $62k is some per-product or department burn, or
  - The “6 months” is a target/assumed runway (not current).
I’ll proceed assuming the conservative case that net burn is higher (i.e., runway is 6 months), but if net burn really is $62k we should revise the plan.

2) Key baseline metrics to gather (if you want a model / slides)
- Gross margin (or COGS) % and dollar amount last quarter.
- Breakdown of operating expenses: R&D, S&M, G&A (monthly).
- Customer concentration (top customers % of revenue).
- Average contract value (ACV), churn or retention, CAC (if available).
- Receivables / payables terms (DSO / DPO).
If you want, I can build a 6-month cash flow and 3-scenario P&L once you confirm these or I can assume/estimate conservative values.

3) Quick financial sanity checks / calculations you can use
- Monthly revenue = $600k.
- If net burn = $200k/month, monthly cash outflows = revenue + net burn = $600k + $200k = $800k.
- If net burn = $62k/month, monthly outflows = $662k/month and runway ≈ 19 months.

4) Margin improvement strategies (practical, prioritized)
Short-term (0–3 months) — quick wins
- Price / packaging tweaks: a 3–5% price increase (or removing legacy discounts) often yields material margin uplift with minimal cost. Example: +5% on $600k = +$30k/month.
- Stop-loss: pause or renegotiate low/no-margin customer deals; move them to defined MVP/paid pilots.
- Reduce variable costs: audit costs tied to usage (cloud, third-party services). A 10–20% cut on waste can be meaningful.
- Cut discretionary spend: freeze non-critical hiring, travel, conferences, and consultant usage.
- Vendor renegotiation / extend payment terms: push DPO out a few weeks to ease cash pressure.
- Collections push: tighten AR follow-up, consider early-pay discounts to improve cash conversion.

Medium-term (3–9 months) — structural improvements
- Improve gross margin: move customers to higher-margin offerings (subscriptions, bundles, professional services that scale). Reprice low-margin offerings.
- Productize / self-serve onboarding: reduce S&M & support per new customer (lowers CAC, improves LTV/CAC).
- Automate repetitive finance & ops tasks (billing, reconciliation) to reduce headcount pressure as you scale.
- Outsource non-core functions or shift contractors to outcome-based pricing.
- Cloud & infra optimization: reserved instances, right-sizing, multi-cloud discounts, eliminate unused resources.

Longer-term (9–18 months) — scalable leverage
- Focus on unit economics: measure contribution margin per customer, CAC payback, and LTV. Invest only in segments that scale with positive unit economics.
- Platformization: create a developer/self-serve layer or API monetization so revenue scales without linear cost growth.
- Enterprise sales motion only where net margins justify cost (field sales, custom integrations).
- Partnerships & channel: use partners/resellers to scale sales with lower fixed S&M cost.

5) Cost-savings and revenue uplift examples (illustrative)
- 5% price increase on $600k/month = +$30k/month = +$360k/year.
- Reduce cloud + 3rd-party spend by 15% on an assumed $100k/month = $15k/month = $180k/year.
- Cut non-essential headcount or hiring freeze saving $20k/month in payroll = $240k/year.
- Moving 10% of revenue from low-margin services to subscription (higher margin) can improve gross margin substantially — quantify after you share gross margin.

6) Scalability levers (what to show leadership)
- Improve gross margin first — higher margin gives breathing room for growth.
- Reduce CAC & shorten payback via self-serve flows and product-led growth.
- Convert one-off services to recurring revenue to stabilize cash flow.
- Standardize contracts and SLAs to reduce custom engineering and support load.
- Measure and report: CAC, LTV, gross margin %, contribution margin per customer, CAC payback months, churn.

7) Finance narrative — suggested structure (one page + appendix)
Executive summary (1–2 bullets)
- Current snapshot: cash $1.2M, monthly revenue $600k, runway X months (clarify), headline margin if known.
- Objective: Extend runway to Y months while launching margin-improving initiatives that preserve growth.

Headline KPIs
- Revenue (LTM or last quarter), monthly run-rate, gross margin %, net burn, cash runway.
- Top 3 risks (e.g., customer concentration, high fixed costs, rising CAC) and mitigations.

Drivers & initiatives (with estimated financial impact and timing)
- Quick wins (0–3 months): pricing, vendor renegotiation, hiring freeze — estimated $X/month.
- Medium (3–9 months): productization, automation — expected margin uplift and CAC improvements.
- Long-term (9–18 months): platformization, channels — scalable revenue.

Financial projection & ask
- Show 3 scenarios (Base / Conservative / Upside) for 6–12 months: cash balance, net burn, runway.
- If additional funding is required: amount, use of funds, runway extension, milestones.

Call to action / next steps
- Approve 30-day quick-win list (list specific cuts and revenue moves).
- Approve building a 6-month cash-flow and P&L model (I can build this).
- Weekly checkpoint to track realized savings vs target.

Appendix
- Sensitivity tables and detailed assumptions.
- Initiative owners, milestones, and KPI targets.

8) Suggested immediate 7–day action plan
Day 1–2: Validate cash flows and reconcile the burn discrepancy (I can prepare a short worksheet).
Day 3–5: Implement quick wins — pricing notices, pause hiring, vendor renegotiations, cloud cost audit.
Day 6–7: Run 3-scenario 6-month model and prepare the one-page finance narrative slide for leadership.

9) Offer: I can help build the deliverables you need
- I can draft:
  - A one-page finance narrative / executive summary slide text.
  - A 6-month cash-flow model with Base / Conservative / Upside scenarios (using your confirmed burn or using conservative assumptions).
  - A short memo listing quick-win items with owners and estimated savings.
Tell me which you want first. If you want the model, please confirm whether net burn is $62k or $200k (or give actual monthly expense / COGS numbers). If you prefer, I can proceed using conservative assumptions (runway = 6 months / net burn = $200k) and produce a model + narrative.

Which deliverable should I prepare now — the one-page finance narrative, the 6-month cash model, or a slide-ready bullet list of quick wins + owners?

