Which of these is correct: the 62K burn, the 6‑month runway, or something else (e.g., 62K is net burn vs. 200K is gross burn before revenue)? Also confirm whether the $1.8M revenue last quarter is recognized revenue (GAAP) and whether it’s recurring. Once you confirm, I’ll produce numbers and scenarios that match.

Below I’ll assume both possibilities and give immediate, near‑term, and strategic recommendations plus a short finance narrative you can use or adapt.

Quick baseline math (from your provided numbers)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter $1.8M) = $1.8M / 3 = $600K/month. Run‑rate revenue ≈ $7.2M/year.
- Scenario A (burn = $62K/month): runway = ~19.4 months; company is cash-positive relative to revenue.
- Scenario B (runway = 6 months): implied burn ≈ $200K/month (net cash outflow).

Key KPIs to include in any finance narrative
- Cash on hand, runway (months), monthly burn (gross and net)
- Monthly recurring revenue (MRR) or run-rate revenue
- Gross margin % and contribution margin by product/service
- EBITDA and EBITDA margin
- CAC, LTV, payback period (if applicable)
- AR days, customer concentration (top 5 customers %), churn (%)
- CapEx and near-term financing needs

Immediate actions (0–30 days) — preserve runway and shore up liquidity
- Reconcile numbers and confirm runway calculation (urgent).
- Freeze hiring and nonessential spend.
- Prioritize collections: invoice aging, offer early‑pay discounts for cash, push on receivables.
- Renegotiate vendor contracts and payment terms (push out payables where possible).
- Pause or reallocate marketing spend to highest-ROI channels.
- Convert short-term receivables to cash (factoring or short-term line) only if necessary.
- Prepare a 30/60/90 cash flow forecast and identify the breakeven month.

Near term (1–6 months) — margin improvement and quick revenue levers
- Reduce COGS: negotiate supplier rates, consolidate vendors, shift to higher-margin suppliers or negotiated CPMs/fees.
- Pricing optimization: test price increases or packaging changes for higher ARPU; move clients to value-based pricing where possible.
- Productize services: turn bespoke work into packaged offerings with standard pricing and SOW to improve margins and predictability.
- Shift revenue mix to recurring/subscription where possible (improves predictability and valuation).
- Increase automation and outsource noncore functions to lower fixed costs.
- Cross-sell and upsell to existing customers (higher ROI than new acquisition).
- Improve utilization: reduce bench time and improve billable utilization for services teams.

Medium / long-term (6–24 months) — scalability and sustainable margin expansion
- Platform or technology investments that reduce marginal cost per unit of revenue (automation, data platforms).
- Focus on high-margin products and scale them (digital products, data services, licensing, subscription).
- Strategic partnerships and channel expansion to scale distribution without proportional SG&A increase.
- Centralize finance, ops, and procurement to enforce discipline and capture economies of scale.
- Consider small bolt-on acquisitions to add high-margin capabilities or repeatable revenue.
- Implement unit economics tracking for each product/segment (CAC, gross margin, contribution margin).

Simple scenario examples (illustrative — I’ll build exact tables once you confirm numbers)
- If burn is $62K/month: a 15% cost reduction saves $9.3K/month -> extends runway from ~19.4 to ~22.9 months.
- If implied burn is $200K/month: a 25% cut to discretionary spend saves $50K/month -> extends runway from 6 to ~9 months (200K → 150K burn).
- Revenue upsell: increasing average client spend by 10% on $600K monthly revenue = +$60K/month — can materially change runway and break-even.

Suggested finance narrative (one-page executive summary — edit to fit tone)
- Opening: “Horizon Media enters the next quarter with $1.2M in cash and a run-rate revenue of ~$7.2M/year ($600K/month). Our focus is extending runway while improving margins through immediate cash preservation and medium‑term portfolio shifts toward higher‑margin, recurring revenue.”
- Current state and ask: “We have two levers: (1) reduce near-term cash burn and (2) accelerate margin expansion through pricing, productization, and operational improvement. We propose a 30/60/90 plan to secure runway and a 12‑month plan to improve gross margins by X–Y pts.”
- Key actions: bullet list of Immediate / Near / Long items (from above).
- KPIs & targets: “Target: extend runway to ≥12 months (if needed) within 90 days; improve gross margin by 5–10 points in 12 months; reduce AR days to <45; CAC payback within 12 months.”
- Ask / Resources: funding need or approvals needed (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiation mandate, budget for priority automation), and who owns each action.

What I can do next (pick one)
- I can draft a 1‑page investor/CFO memo or an internal slide deck outline with numbers and scenarios — I’ll need you to confirm which burn/runway number is correct and provide gross margin or major cost buckets (payroll, media buying, S&M, G&A).
- Or I can build a 3-scenario P&L/cash-flow model (base/worst/best) showing runway and when cash hits zero under different actions — I’ll need monthly revenue and expense breakdown (or I can make reasonable assumptions if you prefer).

Which would you like me to prepare now, and can you confirm the true monthly burn or clarify the 6‑month figure? If you want the memo/slide deck, tell me the audience (CFO, Board, investors) and tone (concise/urgent vs. strategic).

