Good — I can help. First, a quick data check, then practical, prioritized recommendations, a suggested finance narrative you can use in internal materials, the KPIs to show, and next steps (I can also draft slides or a model if you want).

1) Quick data check (need you to confirm)
- You said: cash = $1.2M, runway = 6 months, burn = $62K/month.
- Those three don’t line up: runway = cash / burn. If burn is $62K/month, 1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months of runway (not 6). If runway is actually 6 months and burn is correct, cash would be ~372K (6 × 62K). Which is correct: cash = $1.2M or runway = 6 months? Also confirm whether “burn” is net cash burn after operating revenue (i.e., cash outflows minus cash inflows) or just operating expense burn (cash outflows before revenue). I’ll assume for now the correct inputs are cash = $1.2M and net burn = $62K/month unless you tell me otherwise.

2) High-level summary and objectives
- Current snapshot (example, assuming cash = $1.2M): healthy revenue last quarter ($1.8M), modest net burn ($62K/mo) and >18 months runway. Objectives for your narrative: (a) improve margins (gross and operating) to increase free cash flow, (b) scale revenue efficiently (improve unit economics), and (c) show clear plan and milestones to the board/leadership for runway extension, growth, or fundraising readiness.

3) Prioritized actions (by timing and impact)

Quick wins (0–3 months)
- Reconcile unit economics by product/customer: calculate gross margin per product or service, contribution margin, and highest/lowest margin customers. Prioritize high-margin offers and pause or reprice low-margin work.
- Pricing / packaging tweaks: raise price on low-flexibility contracts or add value-based pricing for premium customers. A 3–5% price lift on top 30% of revenue often improves margins quickly.
- Reduce discretionary spend: freeze non-critical hiring, travel, marketing campaigns with poorly attributed ROI. Target 10–20% cut in discretionary OPEX.
- Vendor renegotiation & procurement: renegotiate top 5 vendor contracts (may yield 5–15% savings).
Estimated impact example: 10% reduction in opex could reduce burn from 62K to ~56K (if OPEX dominates burn) and add ~3 months runway on a 1.2M cash base. If you instead reduce total cash burn by 20% → 62K → ~50K → adds ~3.5–4.5 months runway.

Medium-term (3–12 months)
- Drive revenue mix to higher-margin services/products: upsell existing customers, cross-sell bundles, create add-ons with high gross margin.
- Improve sales efficiency: tighten customer segmentation, shorten sales cycle, focus on top ICP. Improve conversion rates or ARPA (average revenue per account).
- Automate / standardize operations: move repetitive tasks to automation or lower-cost locations; this turns fixed costs into variable, improving scalability.
- Introduce performance-based compensation for sales to push higher-margin deals.

Longer-term (12+ months)
- Productize services where possible to turn labor into scalable offerings (platforms, subscription, packaged solutions).
- Invest in a scalable tech stack that reduces marginal cost per customer.
- Strategic partnerships for distribution to grow revenue without proportional increases in SG&A.

4) Margin & scalability levers (concrete list)
- Gross margin: reduce COGS (outsource, renegotiate suppliers), increase price, improve utilization of billable staff.
- Contribution margin: reduce variable acquisition costs (lower CAC), increase ARPU, improve churn retention.
- Operating margin: reduce fixed costs, restructure expensive cost centers, implement shared services.
- Scalability: standardize onboarding, modular offerings, automation, self-serve flows, API integrations.

5) KPIs and dashboard to include in internal materials
- Cash on hand, monthly burn, runway (explicit formula).
- Revenue (quarter and LTM), QoQ growth rate, ARR/NRR if subscription elements exist.
- Gross margin % and gross profit by product line.
- Contribution margin per customer / account.
- CAC (customer acquisition cost), LTV (lifetime value), LTV:CAC ratio.
- Burn multiple (net burn / net new ARR; if SaaS-like).
- EBITDA / Adjusted EBITDA, operating cash flow.
- Headcount cost per revenue (FTEs and utilization).
- Top 10 customers % of revenue, churn by cohort.

6) Finance narrative outline (short, board-ready)
- Title/headline: “Improving Margins & Scalable Growth — 12-Month Plan”
- 1-slide summary (one-liner): current position, main risk/opportunity, and the ask.
  Example one-liner: “Horizon Media ended Q[ ] with $1.2M cash and strong top-line momentum (Q rev $1.8M). To convert revenue into repeatable, high-margin growth, we will (1) optimize product mix and pricing for margin, (2) eliminate low-return costs, and (3) invest selectively in automation to scale efficiently.”
- Problem statement (1 slide): unit economics gaps, margin leakage, operational scaling barriers.
- Action plan (2–3 slides): Quick wins (expected impact + timeline), Medium-term actions (investments and ROI), Long-term roadmap.
- Financial impact (1 slide): 3 scenarios — Base / Improvement / Stretch with projected runway, margin %, and EBIT/EBITDA changes over 12 months.
- Ask & KPIs (1 slide): resources required (if any), decision points, and top 6 metrics to track weekly/monthly.

7) Sample talking points (for exec summary)
- “We generated $1.8M last quarter, demonstrating strong demand; our goal is to convert this into durable, higher-margin revenue.”
- “Immediate focus: recover margin via pricing and vendor renegotiation, and freeze discretionary spend to protect runway.”
- “Within 3 months we expect to improve operating cash flow by X% through cost and pricing measures (I can model X scenarios once you confirm inputs).”
- “12-month target: raise gross margin by 5–10 points and increase revenue efficiency (LTV:CAC) to >3x, enabling self-funded growth.”

8) Example scenario (illustrative)
Assume (for illustration): cash 1.2M, burn 62K:
- Base: no change → runway ≈ 19.4 months.
- Cost improvement: 15% opex reduction → burn ≈ 53K → runway ≈ 22.6 months.
- Revenue improvement: +10% next quarter (from $1.8M) and +10% gross margin → improves free cash flow further; exact effect depends on margin conversion.
I can build a 12-month P&L/cash flow showing these scenarios if you want.

9) Next steps I can do for you (pick one)
- Draft a 1-page finance narrative and 6-slide board deck using your confirmed numbers (I’ll need COGS, fixed vs variable costs, headcount cost, major vendors).
- Build a 12-month cash-flow model showing 3 scenarios (base / improvement / stretch).
- Produce a one-page KPI dashboard template you can update monthly.
